window.PYREF={"sections":[{"id":"tk-window","title":"Windows","blurb":"Opening, sizing, placing and closing windows","group":"tkinter","n":25},{"id":"tk-label-button","title":"Labels and buttons","blurb":"Showing text, changing it, and running code on click","group":"tkinter","n":23},{"id":"tk-entry-text","title":"Text input","blurb":"Entry boxes and multi-line Text boxes","group":"tkinter","n":20},{"id":"tk-choices","title":"Choices and lists","blurb":"Checkbuttons, radio buttons, dropdowns, listboxes, sliders, spinboxes","group":"tkinter","n":16},{"id":"tk-layout","title":"Layout","blurb":"pack, grid, place, frames, spacing, resizing and scrolling","group":"tkinter","n":20},{"id":"tk-events","title":"Events and keys","blurb":"bind, keyboard, mouse, shortcuts, focus","group":"tkinter","n":17},{"id":"tk-vars","title":"Variables","blurb":"StringVar, IntVar, BooleanVar, trace, sharing values","group":"tkinter","n":7},{"id":"tk-time","title":"Timers, loops and threads","blurb":"after(), animation, clocks, freezing, background work","group":"tkinter","n":10}],"entries":[{"id":"tk.first-window","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make a window in tkinter?","qh":"How do I make a window in tkinter?","a":"Create one <code>Tk()</code> object, add widgets to it, then call <code>mainloop()</code> as the last line.","aka":"create window, open gui, empty window, tk.Tk, hello world gui, start tkinter, blank window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()              <i class=\"c\"># the main window</i>\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;My app&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># text in the title bar</i>\n\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello!&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\n\nroot.mainloop()             <i class=\"c\"># keeps the window open and responding</i></pre></figure><p><code>root</code> is just a name; <code>window</code> or <code>app</code> works the same. Make only <b>one</b> <code>Tk()</code> per program. For extra windows use <a href=\"#/a/tk.toplevel\">Toplevel</a>.</p>","see":["tk.mainloop","tk.import-style","tk.geometry"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() # the main window root.title( \"My app\" ) # text in the title bar tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello!\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() # keeps the window open and responding root is just a name; window or app works the same. Make only one Tk() per program. For extra windows use Toplevel ."},{"id":"tk.mainloop","s":"tk-window","q":"Why does my window open and close instantly (or never show)?","qh":"Why does my window open and close instantly (or never show)?","a":"You're missing <code>root.mainloop()</code> at the end, or something after it never gets reached. <code>mainloop()</code> is what keeps the window alive.","aka":"window disappears, window not showing, mainloop missing, gui closes immediately, nothing happens when I run","err":"","h":"<p><code>mainloop()</code> waits for clicks and key presses and redraws the window. Code <b>after</b> it only runs once the window is closed.</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Still here&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;This prints after you close the window&quot;</i>)</pre></figure><p>Other causes:</p><ul><li>Double-clicking a <code>.pyw</code>/<code>.py</code> file that crashes. Run it from a terminal or IDLE so you can see the error.</li><li>An infinite <code>while True:</code> loop before <code>mainloop()</code>. Use <a href=\"#/a/tk.after\">after()</a> for repeating work instead.</li></ul>","see":["tk.first-window","tk.after-not-sleep"],"t":"mainloop() waits for clicks and key presses and redraws the window. Code after it only runs once the window is closed. import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Still here\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() print ( \"This prints after you close the window\" ) Other causes: Double-clicking a .pyw / .py file that crashes. Run it from a terminal or IDLE so you can see the error. An infinite while True: loop before mainloop() . Use after() for repeating work instead."},{"id":"tk.import-style","s":"tk-window","q":"Should I use import tkinter as tk or from tkinter import *?","qh":"Should I use <code>import tkinter as tk</code> or <code>from tkinter import *</code>?","a":"Prefer <code>import tkinter as tk</code> (and <code>from tkinter import ttk</code>). It keeps names clear and avoids clashes. <code>from tkinter import *</code> works but hides where names come from.","aka":"import tkinter, from tkinter import star, tkinter vs Tkinter, ttk import, import ttk","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk, messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nttk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: messagebox.showinfo(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><ul><li>Python 3: <code>tkinter</code> (lowercase). Python 2 used <code>Tkinter</code>. If a tutorial says <code>import Tkinter</code>, it is very old.</li><li><code>messagebox</code>, <code>filedialog</code>, <code>ttk</code>, <code>simpledialog</code>, <code>colorchooser</code> are <b>submodules</b>: <code>import tkinter</code> alone does not load them, so <code>tk.messagebox</code> can fail. Import them explicitly.</li></ul>","see":["tk.install","tk.ttk-vs-tk"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk, messagebox root = tk.Tk() ttk.Button(root, text= \"Hi\" , command= lambda : messagebox.showinfo( \"Hi\" , \"Hello\" )).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() Python 3: tkinter (lowercase). Python 2 used Tkinter . If a tutorial says import Tkinter , it is very old. messagebox , filedialog , ttk , simpledialog , colorchooser are submodules : import tkinter alone does not load them, so tk.messagebox can fail. Import them explicitly."},{"id":"tk.install","s":"tk-window","q":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'. How do I install it?","qh":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'. How do I install it?","a":"tkinter comes with Python; you don't <code>pip install</code> it. On Windows/macOS re-run the python.org installer and tick \"tcl/tk and IDLE\". On Linux install the system package.","aka":"install tkinter, pip install tkinter, tkinter missing, no module named _tkinter, tkinter not found linux, tkinter mac","err":"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'tkinter'|No module named '_tkinter'|ImportError: No module named Tkinter","h":"<figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre># Debian / Ubuntu / Raspberry Pi OS\nsudo apt install python3-tk\n# Fedora\nsudo dnf install python3-tkinter\n# Arch\nsudo pacman -S tk\n# macOS with Homebrew Python\nbrew install python-tk</pre></figure><p>Test it:</p><figure class=\"code sh\" data-tag=\"terminal\"><pre>python3 -m tkinter</pre></figure><p>A small window with a \"Click me!\" button means tkinter works. <code>pip install tkinter</code> always fails because it isn't on PyPI.</p>","see":["tk.import-style","tk.file-named-tkinter"],"t":"# Debian / Ubuntu / Raspberry Pi OS sudo apt install python3-tk # Fedora sudo dnf install python3-tkinter # Arch sudo pacman -S tk # macOS with Homebrew Python brew install python-tk Test it: python3 -m tkinter A small window with a \"Click me!\" button means tkinter works. pip install tkinter always fails because it isn't on PyPI."},{"id":"tk.file-named-tkinter","s":"tk-window","q":"AttributeError: module 'tkinter' has no attribute 'Tk'","qh":"AttributeError: module 'tkinter' has no attribute 'Tk'","a":"You named your own file <code>tkinter.py</code> (or there's a <code>tkinter</code> folder next to it), so Python imports <b>your</b> file instead of the real one. Rename it, and delete any <code>__pycache__</code> folder beside it.","aka":"partially initialized module tkinter, tkinter has no attribute, circular import tkinter, my file is called tkinter.py","err":"AttributeError: module 'tkinter' has no attribute|partially initialized module 'tkinter'","h":"<p>The same happens with <code>random.py</code>, <code>math.py</code>, <code>turtle.py</code>, <code>time.py</code>, <code>socket.py</code>... Never name a script after a module you import.</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(tkinter.__file__)   <i class=\"c\"># shows which file was really imported</i></pre></figure>","see":["py.err.circular-import"],"t":"The same happens with random.py , math.py , turtle.py , time.py , socket.py ... Never name a script after a module you import. import tkinter print (tkinter.__file__) # shows which file was really imported"},{"id":"tk.title","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I change the window title?","qh":"How do I change the window title?","a":"<code>root.title(&quot;New title&quot;)</code>. Call it again any time to change it.","aka":"title bar text, rename window, window name, set title","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Score: 0&quot;</i>)\nscore = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> score\n    score += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    root.title(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Score: </i>{score}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=add).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Score: 0\" ) score = 0 def add (): global score score += 1 root.title( f\" Score: {score} \" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+1\" , command=add).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.geometry","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I set the window size?","qh":"How do I set the window size?","a":"<code>root.geometry(&quot;400x300&quot;)</code> sets width x height in pixels. Add <code>+x+y</code> to also set its position: <code>&quot;400x300+100+50&quot;</code>.","aka":"window size, width height, geometry, resize window, set window dimensions, 800x600","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;400x300&quot;</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># width x height</i>\n<i class=\"c\"># root.geometry(&quot;400x300+200+100&quot;)  # ... and 200px from left, 100px from top</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;400 x 300&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Without <code>geometry()</code> the window shrinks to fit its widgets. Note the <code>x</code> is a lowercase letter, not <code>*</code>.</p>","see":["tk.center","tk.resizable","tk.minsize","tk.fullscreen"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"400x300\" ) # width x height # root.geometry(\"400x300+200+100\") # ... and 200px from left, 100px from top tk.Label(root, text= \"400 x 300\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop() Without geometry() the window shrinks to fit its widgets. Note the x is a lowercase letter, not * ."},{"id":"tk.center","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I center the window on the screen?","qh":"How do I center the window on the screen?","a":"Read the screen size with <code>winfo_screenwidth()</code>/<code>winfo_screenheight()</code>, work out the top-left corner, and pass it to <code>geometry()</code>.","aka":"centre window, middle of screen, center gui, position window center","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">center</i>(win, w, h):\n    sw = win.winfo_screenwidth()\n    sh = win.winfo_screenheight()\n    x = (sw - w) // <i class=\"n\">2</i>\n    y = (sh - h) // <i class=\"n\">2</i>\n    win.geometry(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{w}<i class=\"s\">x</i>{h}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{x}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncenter(root, <i class=\"n\">420</i>, <i class=\"n\">260</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Centered&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If you don't know the size in advance, call <code>win.update_idletasks()</code> first, then use <code>win.winfo_width()</code> / <code>winfo_height()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.geometry","tk.screen-size"],"t":"import tkinter as tk def center (win, w, h): sw = win.winfo_screenwidth() sh = win.winfo_screenheight() x = (sw - w) // 2 y = (sh - h) // 2 win.geometry( f\" {w} x {h} + {x} + {y} \" ) root = tk.Tk() center(root, 420 , 260 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Centered\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop() If you don't know the size in advance, call win.update_idletasks() first, then use win.winfo_width() / winfo_height() ."},{"id":"tk.screen-size","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I get the screen width and height?","qh":"How do I get the screen width and height?","a":"<code>root.winfo_screenwidth()</code> and <code>root.winfo_screenheight()</code> return pixels.","aka":"monitor size, screen resolution, display size, winfo_screenwidth","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nw, h = root.winfo_screenwidth(), root.winfo_screenheight()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Your screen is </i>{w}<i class=\"s\"> x </i>{h}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>With display scaling on Windows (e.g. 150%), the numbers may be the scaled size. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.blurry-windows\">Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows?</a>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() w, h = root.winfo_screenwidth(), root.winfo_screenheight() tk.Label(root, text= f\" Your screen is {w} x {h} \" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() With display scaling on Windows (e.g. 150%), the numbers may be the scaled size. See Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows? ."},{"id":"tk.resizable","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I stop the user resizing the window?","qh":"How do I stop the user resizing the window?","a":"<code>root.resizable(False, False)</code> (width, height). This also greys out maximize on Windows.","aka":"fixed size window, disable resize, disable maximize, lock window size, resizable false","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\nroot.resizable(<i class=\"k\">False</i>, <i class=\"k\">False</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Can&#x27;t resize me&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.minsize","tk.geometry"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) root.resizable( False , False ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Can't resize me\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.minsize","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I set a minimum or maximum window size?","qh":"How do I set a minimum or maximum window size?","a":"<code>root.minsize(w, h)</code> and <code>root.maxsize(w, h)</code>.","aka":"minsize, maxsize, smallest window size, limit resize","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.minsize(<i class=\"n\">300</i>, <i class=\"n\">200</i>)\nroot.maxsize(<i class=\"n\">800</i>, <i class=\"n\">600</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Resize me, within limits&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.minsize( 300 , 200 ) root.maxsize( 800 , 600 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Resize me, within limits\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.fullscreen","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make the window fullscreen or maximized?","qh":"How do I make the window fullscreen or maximized?","a":"<code>root.attributes(&quot;-fullscreen&quot;, True)</code> for true fullscreen. For maximized use <code>root.state(&quot;zoomed&quot;)</code> on Windows/macOS or <code>root.attributes(&quot;-zoomed&quot;, True)</code> on Linux.","aka":"full screen, maximize window, zoomed, kiosk mode, F11 fullscreen, escape exit fullscreen","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;F11 toggles fullscreen, Esc leaves&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    root.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-fullscreen&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">not</i> root.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-fullscreen&quot;</i>))\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;F11&gt;&quot;</i>, toggle)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Escape&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: root.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-fullscreen&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">False</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Always give the user a key to leave fullscreen; there is no title bar to close it.</p>","see":["tk.bind-keys"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"F11 toggles fullscreen, Esc leaves\" ).pack(expand= True ) def toggle (event= None ): root.attributes( \"-fullscreen\" , not root.attributes( \"-fullscreen\" )) root.bind( \"<F11>\" , toggle) root.bind( \"<Escape>\" , lambda e: root.attributes( \"-fullscreen\" , False )) root.mainloop() Always give the user a key to leave fullscreen; there is no title bar to close it."},{"id":"tk.icon","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I change the window icon?","qh":"How do I change the window icon?","a":"Use <code>root.iconphoto(True, tk.PhotoImage(file=&quot;icon.png&quot;))</code>, which works on all systems. <code>root.iconbitmap(&quot;app.ico&quot;)</code> works with <code>.ico</code> files on Windows only.","aka":"window icon, iconbitmap, iconphoto, taskbar icon, feather icon, ico file","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nicon = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;icon.png&quot;</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># keep this variable around</i>\nroot.iconphoto(<i class=\"k\">True</i>, icon)              <i class=\"c\"># True = also for later windows</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;New icon&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>On Windows the taskbar may still show the Python icon when run as a script; it shows correctly once packaged (e.g. with PyInstaller <code>--icon</code>).</p>","see":["tk.photoimage"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() icon = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"icon.png\" ) # keep this variable around root.iconphoto( True , icon) # True = also for later windows tk.Label(root, text= \"New icon\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() On Windows the taskbar may still show the Python icon when run as a script; it shows correctly once packaged (e.g. with PyInstaller --icon )."},{"id":"tk.bg-color","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I change the background colour of the window?","qh":"How do I change the background colour of the window?","a":"<code>root.configure(bg=&quot;lightblue&quot;)</code> (or a hex code like <code>&quot;#223344&quot;</code>). Widgets keep their own background, so set <code>bg</code> on them too.","aka":"window background, bg color, background color, root color, configure bg","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\nroot.configure(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#1e2a38&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Dark&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#1e2a38&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>ttk widgets ignore <code>bg=</code>; style them with <a href=\"#/a/tk.ttk-style\">ttk.Style</a>.</p>","see":["tk.colors","tk.ttk-style"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) root.configure(bg= \"#1e2a38\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Dark\" , bg= \"#1e2a38\" , fg= \"white\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop() ttk widgets ignore bg= ; style them with ttk.Style ."},{"id":"tk.topmost","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I keep the window always on top?","qh":"How do I keep the window always on top?","a":"<code>root.attributes(&quot;-topmost&quot;, True)</code>.","aka":"always on top, stay on top, topmost, float above other windows, pin window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-topmost&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I stay on top&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To bring a window to the front once, call <code>root.lift()</code> then <code>root.focus_force()</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.attributes( \"-topmost\" , True ) tk.Label(root, text= \"I stay on top\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) root.mainloop() To bring a window to the front once, call root.lift() then root.focus_force() ."},{"id":"tk.alpha","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make a window transparent?","qh":"How do I make a window transparent?","a":"<code>root.attributes(&quot;-alpha&quot;, 0.8)</code> sets the opacity of the whole window (0 = invisible, 1 = solid).","aka":"transparency, opacity, see through window, alpha, transparent background","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.attributes(<i class=\"s\">&quot;-alpha&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0.85</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;85% opaque&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Making only the <b>background</b> see-through (not the widgets) is Windows-only: <code>root.attributes(&quot;-transparentcolor&quot;, &quot;white&quot;)</code> makes every pure white pixel transparent.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.attributes( \"-alpha\" , 0.85 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"85% opaque\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() Making only the background see-through (not the widgets) is Windows-only: root.attributes(\"-transparentcolor\", \"white\") makes every pure white pixel transparent."},{"id":"tk.no-titlebar","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I remove the title bar (borderless window)?","qh":"How do I remove the title bar (borderless window)?","a":"<code>root.overrideredirect(True)</code>. You then have to provide your own close button and dragging.","aka":"overrideredirect, no border, frameless window, hide title bar, custom title bar, splash screen","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.overrideredirect(<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;260x120+300+200&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;No title bar&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Close&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Good for splash screens. Such a window won't appear in the taskbar on Windows.</p>","see":["tk.drag-window"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.overrideredirect( True ) root.geometry( \"260x120+300+200\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"No title bar\" ).pack(expand= True ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Close\" , command=root.destroy).pack(pady= 8 ) root.mainloop() Good for splash screens. Such a window won't appear in the taskbar on Windows."},{"id":"tk.drag-window","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I drag a window that has no title bar?","qh":"How do I drag a window that has no title bar?","a":"Remember where the mouse went down, then move the window by the difference on <code>&lt;B1-Motion&gt;</code>.","aka":"move borderless window, drag frameless window, move window with mouse","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.overrideredirect(<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;240x100+300+300&quot;</i>)\nbar = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#333&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nbar.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Close&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>(e):\n    root._dx, root._dy = e.x, e.y\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">move</i>(e):\n    root.geometry(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">+</i>{e.x_root - root._dx}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{e.y_root - root._dy}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nbar.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, start)\nbar.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;B1-Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, move)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.no-titlebar","tk.mouse"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.overrideredirect( True ) root.geometry( \"240x100+300+300\" ) bar = tk.Label(root, text= \"drag me\" , bg= \"#333\" , fg= \"white\" ) bar.pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Close\" , command=root.destroy).pack(pady= 10 ) def start (e): root._dx, root._dy = e.x, e.y def move (e): root.geometry( f\" + {e.x_root - root._dx} + {e.y_root - root._dy} \" ) bar.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , start) bar.bind( \"<B1-Motion>\" , move) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.close-protocol","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I run code when the user clicks the X (close) button?","qh":"How do I run code when the user clicks the X (close) button?","a":"<code>root.protocol(&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;, your_function)</code>. Your function must call <code>root.destroy()</code> if it wants the window to actually close.","aka":"on close, WM_DELETE_WINDOW, confirm exit, are you sure you want to quit, save before closing, close event, intercept close","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> messagebox\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Try closing me&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">on_close</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> messagebox.askokcancel(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Quit&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Really quit?&quot;</i>):\n        root.destroy()\n\nroot.protocol(<i class=\"s\">&quot;WM_DELETE_WINDOW&quot;</i>, on_close)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.quit-vs-destroy","tk.messagebox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import messagebox root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Try closing me\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) def on_close (): if messagebox.askokcancel( \"Quit\" , \"Really quit?\" ): root.destroy() root.protocol( \"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\" , on_close) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.quit-vs-destroy","s":"tk-window","q":"What's the difference between quit() and destroy()? How do I close the window from code?","qh":"What's the difference between <code>quit()</code> and <code>destroy()</code>? How do I close the window from code?","a":"Use <code>root.destroy()</code>. It closes the window and ends <code>mainloop()</code>. <code>root.quit()</code> only stops <code>mainloop()</code> and can leave the window on screen (common in IDLE).","aka":"close window button, exit button, quit button, root.quit, root.destroy, close program, end tkinter","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Exit&quot;</i>, command=root.destroy).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To close a second window only, call <code>that_window.destroy()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.close-protocol"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Exit\" , command=root.destroy).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() To close a second window only, call that_window.destroy() ."},{"id":"tk.toplevel","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I open a second window?","qh":"How do I open a second window?","a":"Use <code>tk.Toplevel(root)</code>. Do not create a second <code>tk.Tk()</code>; it causes strange bugs like \"image doesn't exist\".","aka":"new window, popup window, Toplevel, open another window, child window, multiple windows, second Tk","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Main&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">open_window</i>():\n    win = tk.Toplevel(root)\n    win.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Second&quot;</i>)\n    win.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;220x120&quot;</i>)\n    tk.Label(win, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello from window 2&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n    tk.Button(win, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Close&quot;</i>, command=win.destroy).pack()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open&quot;</i>, command=open_window).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">50</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.modal","tk.err.pyimage","tk.pass-data-windows"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Main\" ) def open_window (): win = tk.Toplevel(root) win.title( \"Second\" ) win.geometry( \"220x120\" ) tk.Label(win, text= \"Hello from window 2\" ).pack(pady= 20 ) tk.Button(win, text= \"Close\" , command=win.destroy).pack() tk.Button(root, text= \"Open\" , command=open_window).pack(padx= 50 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.modal","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I make a popup window that blocks the main window until it's closed (modal)?","qh":"How do I make a popup window that blocks the main window until it's closed (modal)?","a":"In the Toplevel call <code>grab_set()</code> (all clicks go to it), <code>transient(root)</code> (stays on top of root), then <code>root.wait_window(win)</code> if you need to wait for the answer.","aka":"modal dialog, grab_set, wait_window, block main window, dialog box, custom dialog","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nresult = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;(nothing yet)&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ask_name</i>():\n    win = tk.Toplevel(root)\n    win.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>)\n    win.transient(root)\n    win.grab_set()\n    e = tk.Entry(win)\n    e.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n    e.focus()\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">ok</i>():\n        result.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(e.get())\n        win.destroy()\n    tk.Button(win, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=ok).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n    root.wait_window(win)       <i class=\"c\"># code below waits until win closes</i>\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Got:&quot;</i>, result.get())\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ask name&quot;</i>, command=ask_name).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=result).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.toplevel","tk.simpledialog"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() result = tk.StringVar(value= \"(nothing yet)\" ) def ask_name (): win = tk.Toplevel(root) win.title( \"Name\" ) win.transient(root) win.grab_set() e = tk.Entry(win) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) e.focus() def ok (): result. set (e.get()) win.destroy() tk.Button(win, text= \"OK\" , command=ok).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.wait_window(win) # code below waits until win closes print ( \"Got:\" , result.get()) tk.Button(root, text= \"Ask name\" , command=ask_name).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 20 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=result).pack(pady=( 0 , 20 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.withdraw","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I hide a window and show it again?","qh":"How do I hide a window and show it again?","a":"<code>win.withdraw()</code> hides it, <code>win.deiconify()</code> shows it again. <code>win.iconify()</code> minimizes it.","aka":"hide window, show window, withdraw, deiconify, iconify, minimize window, hide main window, show dialog without main window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nother = tk.Toplevel(root)\ntk.Label(other, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Peekaboo&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> other.winfo_viewable():\n        other.withdraw()\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        other.deiconify()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hide / show&quot;</i>, command=toggle).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To show only a messagebox with no empty main window, call <code>root.withdraw()</code> first.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() other = tk.Toplevel(root) tk.Label(other, text= \"Peekaboo\" ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) def toggle (): if other.winfo_viewable(): other.withdraw() else : other.deiconify() tk.Button(root, text= \"Hide / show\" , command=toggle).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() To show only a messagebox with no empty main window, call root.withdraw() first."},{"id":"tk.blurry-windows","s":"tk-window","q":"Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows?","qh":"Why does my tkinter window look blurry on Windows?","a":"Windows stretches programs that aren't DPI-aware. Tell Windows your program is DPI-aware <b>before</b> creating <code>Tk()</code>.","aka":"blurry text, fuzzy fonts, high dpi, dpi awareness, pixelated tkinter, scaling 150%","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\n<i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;win32&quot;</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">import</i> ctypes\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        ctypes.windll.shcore.SetProcessDpiAwareness(<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> Exception:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sharp text&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Everything will look smaller but crisp. Use <code>root.tk.call(&quot;tk&quot;, &quot;scaling&quot;, 2.0)</code> to scale up if needed.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys if sys.platform == \"win32\" : import ctypes try : ctypes.windll.shcore.SetProcessDpiAwareness( 1 ) except Exception: pass root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Sharp text\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() Everything will look smaller but crisp. Use root.tk.call(\"tk\", \"scaling\", 2.0) to scale up if needed."},{"id":"tk.window-pos","s":"tk-window","q":"How do I get the window's current size and position?","qh":"How do I get the window's current size and position?","a":"<code>winfo_width()</code>, <code>winfo_height()</code>, <code>winfo_x()</code>, <code>winfo_y()</code>. Call <code>update_idletasks()</code> first if the window was just created, or you'll get 1.","aka":"window x y, winfo_x, winfo_width, where is the window, window coordinates","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;320x180+100+100&quot;</i>)\ninfo = tk.Label(root)\ninfo.pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    info.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{root.winfo_width()}<i class=\"s\">x</i>{root.winfo_height()}<i class=\"s\"> at </i>{root.winfo_x()}<i class=\"s\">,</i>{root.winfo_y()}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, show)\nroot.update_idletasks()\nshow()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>root.geometry()</code> with no argument returns the same info as one string like <code>&quot;320x180+100+100&quot;</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"320x180+100+100\" ) info = tk.Label(root) info.pack(expand= True ) def show (event= None ): info.config(text= f\" {root.winfo_width()} x {root.winfo_height()} at {root.winfo_x()} , {root.winfo_y()} \" ) root.bind( \"<Configure>\" , show) root.update_idletasks() show() root.mainloop() root.geometry() with no argument returns the same info as one string like \"320x180+100+100\" ."},{"id":"tk.label","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I show text in a window?","qh":"How do I show text in a window?","a":"Use a <code>Label</code>: <code>tk.Label(root, text=&quot;Hi&quot;).pack()</code>.","aka":"label, display text, show message, print to window, put text on screen, tk.Label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello, world&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Big and red&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>), fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Useful options: <code>text</code>, <code>font</code>, <code>fg</code> (text colour), <code>bg</code>, <code>padx</code>/<code>pady</code> (inside space), <code>width</code> (in characters), <code>anchor</code>, <code>justify</code>, <code>wraplength</code>, <code>image</code>.</p>","see":["tk.label-change","tk.label-font","tk.label-wrap"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello, world\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Big and red\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 20 ), fg= \"red\" ).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Useful options: text , font , fg (text colour), bg , padx / pady (inside space), width (in characters), anchor , justify , wraplength , image ."},{"id":"tk.label-change","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a label's text after it's created?","qh":"How do I change a label's text after it's created?","a":"Keep the label in a variable and call <code>label.config(text=&quot;new&quot;)</code>. Or link it to a <code>StringVar</code> with <code>textvariable=</code>.","aka":"update label, change label text, set label text, label config, refresh label, label not updating, change text of label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlabel = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Not clicked&quot;</i>)\nlabel.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clicked</i>():\n    label.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clicked!&quot;</i>)      <i class=\"c\"># same as label[&quot;text&quot;] = &quot;Clicked!&quot;</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Click&quot;</i>, command=clicked).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If you get <code>&#x27;NoneType&#x27; object has no attribute &#x27;config&#x27;</code>, you wrote <code>label = tk.Label(...).pack()</code>. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.err.none-grid\">AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...)</a>.</p>","see":["tk.err.none-grid","tk.stringvar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() label = tk.Label(root, text= \"Not clicked\" ) label.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) def clicked (): label.config(text= \"Clicked!\" ) # same as label[\"text\"] = \"Clicked!\" tk.Button(root, text= \"Click\" , command=clicked).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() If you get 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'config' , you wrote label = tk.Label(...).pack() . See AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...) ."},{"id":"tk.label-font","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change the font and size of text?","qh":"How do I change the font and size of text?","a":"Pass <code>font=(&quot;Family&quot;, size, &quot;bold&quot;)</code>, e.g. <code>font=(&quot;Helvetica&quot;, 16, &quot;bold&quot;)</code>. Styles: <code>&quot;bold&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;italic&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;underline&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;overstrike&quot;</code>, or several: <code>&quot;bold italic&quot;</code>.","aka":"font size, bigger text, bold text, italic, change font, font family, text size label button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Normal&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>)).pack()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bold 18&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Helvetica&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>)).pack()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Italic underline&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Times&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;italic underline&quot;</i>)).pack()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Mono&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">14</i>)).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If the font isn't installed, Tk silently picks a fallback. A font name with spaces must be inside the tuple: <code>(&quot;Comic Sans MS&quot;, 12)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.font-object","tk.font-list"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Normal\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 12 )).pack() tk.Label(root, text= \"Bold 18\" , font=( \"Helvetica\" , 18 , \"bold\" )).pack() tk.Label(root, text= \"Italic underline\" , font=( \"Times\" , 14 , \"italic underline\" )).pack() tk.Label(root, text= \"Mono\" , font=( \"Courier\" , 14 )).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() If the font isn't installed, Tk silently picks a fallback. A font name with spaces must be inside the tuple: (\"Comic Sans MS\", 12) ."},{"id":"tk.label-wrap","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make long text wrap onto several lines?","qh":"How do I make long text wrap onto several lines?","a":"Use <code>wraplength=</code> (in pixels) on a Label, or put <code>\\n</code> in the text. <code>justify=&quot;left&quot;</code> lines the lines up on the left.","aka":"wrap text, wraplength, multiline label, text goes off screen, newline in label, line break label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlong = <i class=\"s\">&quot;This is a long sentence that would run off the side of a small window if it did not wrap.&quot;</i>\ntk.Label(root, text=long, wraplength=<i class=\"n\">220</i>, justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Line one\\nLine two&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To wrap to whatever width the window is, update <code>wraplength</code> on resize:</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Resize the window and this text rewraps to fit the new width. &quot;</i> * <i class=\"n\">3</i>, justify=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lab.config(wraplength=e.width - <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() long = \"This is a long sentence that would run off the side of a small window if it did not wrap.\" tk.Label(root, text=long, wraplength= 220 , justify= \"left\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Line one\\nLine two\" ).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() To wrap to whatever width the window is, update wraplength on resize: import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Resize the window and this text rewraps to fit the new width. \" * 3 , justify= \"left\" ) lab.pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) lab.bind( \"<Configure>\" , lambda e: lab.config(wraplength=e.width - 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.label-align","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I align text left or right inside a label?","qh":"How do I align text left or right inside a label?","a":"<code>anchor=&quot;w&quot;</code> moves the text to the left of the label's area (<code>&quot;e&quot;</code> right, <code>&quot;center&quot;</code>). <code>justify</code> only lines up multiple lines relative to each other.","aka":"anchor, justify, left align, right align, text alignment, align label text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x150&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>anchor</code> only matters when the label is wider than its text, which is why <code>fill=&quot;x&quot;</code> is used here.</p>","see":["tk.pack-fill","tk.grid-sticky"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x150\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"left\" , anchor= \"w\" , bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"right\" , anchor= \"e\" , bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"center\" , bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) root.mainloop() anchor only matters when the label is wider than its text, which is why fill=\"x\" is used here."},{"id":"tk.button","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make a button that runs a function?","qh":"How do I make a button that runs a function?","a":"Pass the function <b>name without brackets</b> as <code>command=</code>: <code>tk.Button(root, text=&quot;Go&quot;, command=go)</code>.","aka":"button click, button command, onclick, on click event, button callback, button does nothing, call function button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">say_hi</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hi!&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Say hi&quot;</i>, command=say_hi).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>command=say_hi()</code> is wrong: it calls the function once at startup and gives the button its return value (<code>None</code>).</p>","see":["tk.err.command-runs-early","tk.button-args","tk.button-state"],"t":"import tkinter as tk def say_hi (): print ( \"Hi!\" ) root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Say hi\" , command=say_hi).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop() command=say_hi() is wrong: it calls the function once at startup and gives the button its return value ( None )."},{"id":"tk.err.command-runs-early","s":"tk-label-button","q":"Why does my button's function run straight away instead of when I click?","qh":"Why does my button's function run straight away instead of when I click?","a":"You wrote <code>command=func()</code> with brackets. That calls it right away. Write <code>command=func</code>, or <code>command=lambda: func(arg)</code> if you need arguments.","aka":"command runs immediately, function runs on start, button runs once, button command called at startup, button not working on click","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, command=save())   <i class=\"c\"># runs save() now, once</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>(name):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Saving&quot;</i>, name)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: save(<i class=\"s\">&quot;notes.txt&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.button-args","tk.lambda-loop"],"t":"tk.Button(root, text= \"Save\" , command=save()) # runs save() now, once import tkinter as tk def save (name): print ( \"Saving\" , name) root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Save\" , command= lambda : save( \"notes.txt\" )).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-args","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I pass arguments to a button's command?","qh":"How do I pass arguments to a button's command?","a":"Wrap the call in a <code>lambda</code>: <code>command=lambda: add(5)</code>. <code>functools.partial(add, 5)</code> also works.","aka":"button function with parameters, command with arguments, lambda button, partial, pass value to function button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> functools <i class=\"k\">import</i> partial\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntotal = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>(n):\n    total.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(total.get() + n)\n\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=total, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: add(<i class=\"n\">1</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+10&quot;</i>, command=partial(add, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.lambda-loop"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from functools import partial root = tk.Tk() total = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) def add (n): total. set (total.get() + n) tk.Label(root, textvariable=total, font=( \"Arial\" , 20 )).pack(pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+1\" , command= lambda : add( 1 )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"+10\" , command=partial(add, 10 )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.lambda-loop","s":"tk-label-button","q":"I made buttons in a loop and they all do the same thing (the last value). Why?","qh":"I made buttons in a loop and they all do the same thing (the last value). Why?","a":"A lambda remembers the <b>variable</b>, not its value at that moment. Freeze the value with a default argument: <code>command=lambda n=n: pick(n)</code>.","aka":"lambda in loop, buttons all same value, loop variable lambda, late binding, every button prints last number, buttons in for loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    tk.Button(root, text=n, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: pick(n))   <i class=\"c\"># every button gives 4</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nout = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Pick one&quot;</i>)\nout.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">pick</i>(n):\n    out.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">You picked </i>{n}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> n <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(n), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> n=n: pick(n)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.button-args","tk.button-grid"],"t":"for n in range ( 5 ): tk.Button(root, text=n, command= lambda : pick(n)) # every button gives 4 import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() out = tk.Label(root, text= \"Pick one\" ) out.pack() def pick (n): out.config(text= f\" You picked {n} \" ) for n in range ( 5 ): tk.Button(root, text= str (n), width= 4 , command= lambda n=n: pick(n)).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 2 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-grid","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make a grid of buttons (like a keypad or tic-tac-toe board)?","qh":"How do I make a grid of buttons (like a keypad or tic-tac-toe board)?","a":"Create them in nested loops with <code>grid(row=r, column=c)</code> and store them in a list so you can change them later.","aka":"button grid, 3x3 buttons, keypad, many buttons loop, board of buttons, list of buttons","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbuttons = []\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">press</i>(r, c):\n    b = buttons[r][c]\n    b.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;X&quot;</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n    row = []\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n        b = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot; &quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> r=r, c=c: press(r, c))\n        b.grid(row=r, column=c, padx=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n        row.append(b)\n    buttons.append(row)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.lambda-loop","tk.grid","tk.recipe-tictactoe"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() buttons = [] def press (r, c): b = buttons[r][c] b.config(text= \"X\" , state= \"disabled\" ) for r in range ( 3 ): row = [] for c in range ( 3 ): b = tk.Button(root, text= \" \" , width= 6 , height= 3 , command= lambda r=r, c=c: press(r, c)) b.grid(row=r, column=c, padx= 2 , pady= 2 ) row.append(b) buttons.append(row) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-state","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I disable a button (grey it out) and enable it again?","qh":"How do I disable a button (grey it out) and enable it again?","a":"<code>button.config(state=&quot;disabled&quot;)</code> and <code>button.config(state=&quot;normal&quot;)</code>. Works for most widgets (Entry, Text, Checkbutton...).","aka":"disable button, enable button, grey out, state disabled, unclickable button, button state normal","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ngo = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go&quot;</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\ngo.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nagree = tk.BooleanVar()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">changed</i>():\n    go.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;normal&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> agree.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I agree&quot;</i>, variable=agree, command=changed).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For ttk widgets use <code>btn.state([&quot;disabled&quot;])</code> and <code>btn.state([&quot;!disabled&quot;])</code>, or <code>config(state=...)</code>, which also works.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() go = tk.Button(root, text= \"Go\" , state= \"disabled\" ) go.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) agree = tk.BooleanVar() def changed (): go.config(state= \"normal\" if agree.get() else \"disabled\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"I agree\" , variable=agree, command=changed).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() For ttk widgets use btn.state([\"disabled\"]) and btn.state([\"!disabled\"]) , or config(state=...) , which also works."},{"id":"tk.button-size","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a button's size?","qh":"How do I change a button's size?","a":"<code>width</code> and <code>height</code> are in <b>characters/lines</b> for text buttons. For pixel sizes, use <code>padx</code>/<code>pady</code>, or put the button in a Frame of fixed size, or use <code>place(width=, height=)</code>.","aka":"button width height, bigger button, button size pixels, resize button, large button","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;width=20&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">20</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;height=3&quot;</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">3</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;padding&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">12</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;fills the width&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If a button shows an image, <code>width</code>/<code>height</code> are in pixels.</p>","see":["tk.pack-fill"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"width=20\" , width= 20 ).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"height=3\" , height= 3 ).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"padding\" , padx= 30 , pady= 12 ).pack(pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"fills the width\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 4 ) root.mainloop() If a button shows an image, width / height are in pixels."},{"id":"tk.button-color","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a button's colour? (And why doesn't it work on Mac?)","qh":"How do I change a button's colour? (And why doesn't it work on Mac?)","a":"<code>tk.Button(root, bg=&quot;green&quot;, fg=&quot;white&quot;, activebackground=&quot;darkgreen&quot;)</code>. On macOS the native button ignores <code>bg</code>; use a <code>Label</code> that acts like a button, or ttk with the <code>&quot;clam&quot;</code> theme.","aka":"button background color, button bg, button color mac, button fg, activebackground, colored button macos","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Green&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#2e7d32&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, activebackground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#1b5e20&quot;</i>, activeforeground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"c\"># a label that behaves like a button: works everywhere, including macOS</i>\nfake = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Flat button&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">16</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand2&quot;</i>)\nfake.pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nfake.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;clicked&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.hover","tk.ttk-style"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"Green\" , bg= \"#2e7d32\" , fg= \"white\" , activebackground= \"#1b5e20\" , activeforeground= \"white\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) # a label that behaves like a button: works everywhere, including macOS fake = tk.Label(root, text= \"Flat button\" , bg= \"#c23b22\" , fg= \"white\" , padx= 16 , pady= 8 , cursor= \"hand2\" ) fake.pack(pady= 10 ) fake.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"clicked\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.hover","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change a widget's colour when the mouse hovers over it?","qh":"How do I change a widget's colour when the mouse hovers over it?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;Enter&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Leave&gt;</code> and change the colour in each.","aka":"hover effect, mouse over, mouseover, on hover, Enter Leave events, highlight on hover","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nb = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hover me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Enter&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: b.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>))\nb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Leave&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: b.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Plain <code>tk.Button</code> on Windows/Linux also has <code>activebackground</code>, the colour while pressed or hovered.</p>","see":["tk.bind","tk.tooltip"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() b = tk.Label(root, text= \"Hover me\" , bg= \"#ddd\" , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) b.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) b.bind( \"<Enter>\" , lambda e: b.config(bg= \"#ffcc66\" )) b.bind( \"<Leave>\" , lambda e: b.config(bg= \"#ddd\" )) root.mainloop() Plain tk.Button on Windows/Linux also has activebackground , the colour while pressed or hovered."},{"id":"tk.tooltip","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I show a tooltip when hovering over a widget?","qh":"How do I show a tooltip when hovering over a widget?","a":"tkinter has no built-in tooltip. Make a small borderless <code>Toplevel</code> on <code>&lt;Enter&gt;</code> and destroy it on <code>&lt;Leave&gt;</code>.","aka":"tooltip, hint text on hover, hover text, balloon help, popup text mouse over","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">Tooltip</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, widget, text):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.text, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip = widget, text, <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n        widget.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Enter&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.show)\n        widget.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Leave&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.hide)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        x = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget.winfo_rootx() + <i class=\"n\">10</i>\n        y = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget.winfo_rooty() + <i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget.winfo_height() + <i class=\"n\">4</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip = tk.Toplevel(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.widget)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip.overrideredirect(<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip.geometry(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">+</i>{x}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        tk.Label(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip, text=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.text, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffffe0&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;solid&quot;</i>, borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>).pack()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">hide</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip.destroy()\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.tip = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nb = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Save&quot;</i>)\nb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">60</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nTooltip(b, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Save the file (Ctrl+S)&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.hover","tk.no-titlebar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class Tooltip : def __init__ ( self , widget, text): self .widget, self .text, self .tip = widget, text, None widget.bind( \"<Enter>\" , self .show) widget.bind( \"<Leave>\" , self .hide) def show ( self , event= None ): x = self .widget.winfo_rootx() + 10 y = self .widget.winfo_rooty() + self .widget.winfo_height() + 4 self .tip = tk.Toplevel( self .widget) self .tip.overrideredirect( True ) self .tip.geometry( f\" + {x} + {y} \" ) tk.Label( self .tip, text= self .text, bg= \"#ffffe0\" , relief= \"solid\" , borderwidth= 1 , padx= 6 , pady= 2 ).pack() def hide ( self , event= None ): if self .tip: self .tip.destroy() self .tip = None root = tk.Tk() b = tk.Button(root, text= \"Save\" ) b.pack(padx= 60 , pady= 40 ) Tooltip(b, \"Save the file (Ctrl+S)\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-image","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I put an image on a button?","qh":"How do I put an image on a button?","a":"Load a <code>PhotoImage</code> and pass <code>image=</code>. Add <code>compound=&quot;left&quot;</code> to show text beside it. Keep a reference to the image.","aka":"image button, icon button, button with picture, button image and text, compound","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nimg = tk.PhotoImage(file=<i class=\"s\">&quot;icon.png&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, image=img).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot; Open&quot;</i>, image=img, compound=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.img = img      <i class=\"c\"># keep the image alive</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.photoimage","tk.err.image-gone"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() img = tk.PhotoImage(file= \"icon.png\" ) tk.Button(root, image=img).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \" Open\" , image=img, compound= \"left\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) root.img = img # keep the image alive root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.button-enter","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I make the Enter key press a button?","qh":"How do I make the Enter key press a button?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;Return&gt;</code> on the window (or the Entry) to the same function. The function receives an event, so give it <code>event=None</code>.","aka":"enter key button, return key submit, default button, press enter to submit","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ne.focus()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">submit</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Submitted:&quot;</i>, e.get())\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Submit&quot;</i>, command=submit).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, submit)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.bind-keys","tk.entry-get"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) e.focus() def submit (event= None ): print ( \"Submitted:\" , e.get()) tk.Button(root, text= \"Submit\" , command=submit).pack(pady= 10 ) root.bind( \"<Return>\" , submit) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.relief","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change the border style of a widget?","qh":"How do I change the border style of a widget?","a":"Use <code>relief=</code> (<code>&quot;flat&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;raised&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;sunken&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;groove&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;ridge&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;solid&quot;</code>) and <code>borderwidth=</code> (<code>bd=</code>). The focus ring is <code>highlightthickness=</code>.","aka":"relief, border, borderwidth, flat button, sunken, raised, groove, ridge, remove border, highlightthickness","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;flat&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;raised&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;sunken&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;groove&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;ridge&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;solid&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Label(root, text=r, relief=r, borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">12</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;no border&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;flat&quot;</i>, borderwidth=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for r in ( \"flat\" , \"raised\" , \"sunken\" , \"groove\" , \"ridge\" , \"solid\" ): tk.Label(root, text=r, relief=r, borderwidth= 3 , width= 12 , pady= 4 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 3 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"no border\" , relief= \"flat\" , borderwidth= 0 , highlightthickness= 0 ).pack(pady= 8 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.cursor","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I change the mouse cursor over a widget?","qh":"How do I change the mouse cursor over a widget?","a":"<code>cursor=&quot;hand2&quot;</code> on the widget. Others: <code>&quot;arrow&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;watch&quot;</code> (busy), <code>&quot;cross&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;xterm&quot;</code> (text), <code>&quot;fleur&quot;</code> (move), <code>&quot;sb_h_double_arrow&quot;</code>.","aka":"cursor hand, pointer cursor, hand2, busy cursor, watch cursor, change mouse pointer","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;hand2&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;cross&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;cross&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;watch (busy)&quot;</i>, cursor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;watch&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To show \"busy\" for the whole window: <code>root.config(cursor=&quot;watch&quot;)</code>, then <code>root.update()</code>, and set it back to <code>&quot;&quot;</code> afterwards.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"hand\" , cursor= \"hand2\" , pady= 10 ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"cross\" , cursor= \"cross\" , pady= 10 ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"watch (busy)\" , cursor= \"watch\" , pady= 10 ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) root.mainloop() To show \"busy\" for the whole window: root.config(cursor=\"watch\") , then root.update() , and set it back to \"\" afterwards."},{"id":"tk.widget-destroy","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I delete or remove a widget?","qh":"How do I delete or remove a widget?","a":"<code>widget.destroy()</code> removes it for good. To clear a frame: <code>for w in frame.winfo_children(): w.destroy()</code>.","aka":"remove widget, delete label, destroy widget, remove button, clear widget, delete all widgets in frame, clear frame","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = tk.Frame(root)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">4</i>):\n    tk.Label(box, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">item </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>).pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clear</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> w <i class=\"k\">in</i> box.winfo_children():\n        w.destroy()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=clear).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.forget"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() box = tk.Frame(root) box.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) for i in range ( 4 ): tk.Label(box, text= f\" item {i} \" ).pack() def clear (): for w in box.winfo_children(): w.destroy() tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command=clear).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.forget","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I hide a widget and show it again later?","qh":"How do I hide a widget and show it again later?","a":"<code>pack_forget()</code> / <code>grid_forget()</code> hide it (it still exists). Show it again with <code>pack()</code> / <code>grid()</code>. <code>grid_remove()</code> remembers its grid options so a plain <code>grid()</code> puts it back.","aka":"hide widget, show widget, pack_forget, grid_forget, grid_remove, toggle visibility, make invisible","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsecret = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Now you see me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>)\nsecret.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> secret.winfo_ismapped():\n        secret.grid_remove()\n    <i class=\"k\">else</i>:\n        secret.grid()\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Toggle&quot;</i>, command=toggle).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>With <code>pack</code>, the widget may come back in a different place. Pack it again with the same options, or use <code>before=</code>/<code>after=</code>.</p>","see":["tk.widget-destroy"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() secret = tk.Label(root, text= \"Now you see me\" , bg= \"#ffcc66\" ) secret.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def toggle (): if secret.winfo_ismapped(): secret.grid_remove() else : secret.grid() tk.Button(root, text= \"Toggle\" , command=toggle).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() With pack , the widget may come back in a different place. Pack it again with the same options, or use before= / after= ."},{"id":"tk.widget-config-get","s":"tk-label-button","q":"How do I read a widget option, like its current text or colour?","qh":"How do I read a widget option, like its current text or colour?","a":"<code>widget.cget(&quot;text&quot;)</code> or <code>widget[&quot;text&quot;]</code>. Set with <code>widget.config(text=...)</code> or <code>widget[&quot;text&quot;] = ...</code>.","aka":"cget, get label text, widget option value, read button text, label text get","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Hello&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(lab.cget(<i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>), lab[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>])\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(lab.keys()[:<i class=\"n\">6</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;...&quot;</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># every option this widget has</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Hello\" , fg= \"blue\" ) lab.pack(padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) print (lab.cget( \"text\" ), lab[ \"fg\" ]) print (lab.keys()[: 6 ], \"...\" ) # every option this widget has root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.message-widget","s":"tk-label-button","q":"What's the Message widget?","qh":"What's the Message widget?","a":"<code>tk.Message</code> is like a Label that wraps text automatically, keeping a width/height ratio (<code>aspect</code>). Most people just use a Label with <code>wraplength</code>.","aka":"tk.Message, message widget, multiline text display","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Message(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;A Message widget wraps long text on its own, aiming for a shape set by aspect.&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">200</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.label-wrap"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Message(root, text= \"A Message widget wraps long text on its own, aiming for a shape set by aspect.\" , width= 200 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-get","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get what the user typed in an Entry box?","qh":"How do I get what the user typed in an Entry box?","a":"Call <code>entry.get()</code> <b>when it's needed</b> (e.g. in a button's function), not right after creating the Entry, when it's still empty.","aka":"entry get, read input, get text from entry, textbox value, input box, user input gui, entry value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">25</i>)\nname.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nout = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\nout.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">greet</i>():\n    out.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Hello, </i>{name.get()}<i class=\"s\">!</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Greet&quot;</i>, command=greet).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>get()</code> always returns a <b>string</b>. See <a href=\"#/a/tk.entry-number\">How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)?</a> for numbers.</p>","see":["tk.entry-number","tk.err.none-grid","tk.button-enter"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.Entry(root, width= 25 ) name.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) out = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) out.pack() def greet (): out.config(text= f\" Hello, {name.get()} ! \" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Greet\" , command=greet).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() get() always returns a string . See How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)? for numbers."},{"id":"tk.entry-number","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)?","qh":"How do I get a number from an Entry (and handle bad input)?","a":"Convert with <code>int()</code> or <code>float()</code> inside <code>try</code>/<code>except ValueError</code> so letters or an empty box don't crash the program.","aka":"entry int, convert entry to integer, entry float, ValueError entry, invalid literal for int entry, number input tkinter, calculator input","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\na = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>); a.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nb = tk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>); b.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nresult = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\nresult.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        total = <i class=\"b\">float</i>(a.get()) + <i class=\"b\">float</i>(b.get())\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> ValueError:\n        result.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Please type two numbers&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    result.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">= </i>{total:<i class=\"s\">g</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Add&quot;</i>, command=add).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.entry-validate","py.err.valueerror-int"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() a = tk.Entry(root, width= 8 ); a.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 5 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"+\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 ) b = tk.Entry(root, width= 8 ); b.grid(row= 0 , column= 2 , padx= 5 ) result = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) result.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 ) def add (): try : total = float (a.get()) + float (b.get()) except ValueError: result.config(text= \"Please type two numbers\" , fg= \"red\" ) return result.config(text= f\" = {total: g } \" , fg= \"black\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Add\" , command=add).grid(row= 2 , column= 0 , columnspan= 3 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-set","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I put text into an Entry, or clear it?","qh":"How do I put text into an Entry, or clear it?","a":"Clear with <code>entry.delete(0, &quot;end&quot;)</code>, insert with <code>entry.insert(0, &quot;text&quot;)</code>. Or use a <code>StringVar</code>: <code>var.set(&quot;text&quot;)</code>.","aka":"entry insert, set entry text, clear entry, delete entry text, reset input box, empty textbox, entry default value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ne.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;default value&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: e.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fill&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: (e.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>), e.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;filled&quot;</i>))).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>&quot;end&quot;</code> (or <code>tk.END</code>) means \"after the last character\".</p>","see":["tk.stringvar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) e.insert( 0 , \"default value\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : e.delete( 0 , \"end\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Fill\" , command= lambda : (e.delete( 0 , \"end\" ), e.insert( 0 , \"filled\" ))).pack(side= \"left\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() \"end\" (or tk.END ) means \"after the last character\"."},{"id":"tk.entry-password","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make a password box that shows stars?","qh":"How do I make a password box that shows stars?","a":"<code>tk.Entry(root, show=&quot;*&quot;)</code>. Set <code>show=&quot;&quot;</code> to reveal it again.","aka":"password entry, hide input, show asterisks, mask text, show password checkbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\npw = tk.Entry(root, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>)\npw.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nvisible = tk.BooleanVar()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">toggle</i>():\n    pw.config(show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> visible.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Show password&quot;</i>, variable=visible, command=toggle).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.recipe-login"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() pw = tk.Entry(root, show= \"*\" ) pw.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) visible = tk.BooleanVar() def toggle (): pw.config(show= \"\" if visible.get() else \"*\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Show password\" , variable=visible, command=toggle).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-placeholder","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add placeholder (hint) text to an Entry?","qh":"How do I add placeholder (hint) text to an Entry?","a":"tkinter has no placeholder option. Insert grey text, and remove it when the box gets focus (<code>&lt;FocusIn&gt;</code>); put it back on <code>&lt;FocusOut&gt;</code> if empty.","aka":"placeholder, hint text, greyed text in entry, watermark text, default text that disappears, ghost text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">HintEntry</i>(tk.Entry):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, hint, **kw):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master, **kw)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.hint = hint\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.normal = <i class=\"k\">self</i>[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>]\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusIn&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>._in)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusOut&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>._out)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>._out()\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">_in</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, e=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>] == <i class=\"s\">&quot;grey&quot;</i>:\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.config(fg=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.normal)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">_out</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, e=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"b\">super</i>().get():\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>.hint)\n            <i class=\"k\">self</i>.config(fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;grey&quot;</i>)\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">get</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>):\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">self</i>[<i class=\"s\">&quot;fg&quot;</i>] == <i class=\"s\">&quot;grey&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"b\">super</i>().get()\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nHintEntry(root, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Your name&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nHintEntry(root, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Your email&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk class HintEntry (tk.Entry): def __init__ ( self , master, hint, **kw): super ().__init__(master, **kw) self .hint = hint self .normal = self [ \"fg\" ] self .bind( \"<FocusIn>\" , self ._in) self .bind( \"<FocusOut>\" , self ._out) self ._out() def _in ( self , e= None ): if self [ \"fg\" ] == \"grey\" : self .delete( 0 , \"end\" ) self .config(fg= self .normal) def _out ( self , e= None ): if not super ().get(): self .insert( 0 , self .hint) self .config(fg= \"grey\" ) def get ( self ): return \"\" if self [ \"fg\" ] == \"grey\" else super ().get() root = tk.Tk() HintEntry(root, \"Your name\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) HintEntry(root, \"Your email\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-validate","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I only allow numbers (or limit length) in an Entry?","qh":"How do I only allow numbers (or limit length) in an Entry?","a":"Use <code>validate=&quot;key&quot;</code> with a <code>validatecommand</code> that returns <code>True</code> to allow the change. <code>%P</code> passes what the text <b>would</b> become.","aka":"validate entry, numbers only, digits only, restrict input, max length entry, validatecommand, vcmd, character limit","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">only_digits</i>(new_text):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> new_text == <i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> new_text.isdigit()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">max_five</i>(new_text):\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"b\">len</i>(new_text) &lt;= <i class=\"n\">5</i>\n\nvd = root.register(only_digits)\nvl = root.register(max_five)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Digits only&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Entry(root, validate=<i class=\"s\">&quot;key&quot;</i>, validatecommand=(vd, <i class=\"s\">&quot;%P&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Max 5 characters&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Entry(root, validate=<i class=\"s\">&quot;key&quot;</i>, validatecommand=(vl, <i class=\"s\">&quot;%P&quot;</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For decimals use a test like: try <code>float(new_text)</code> and return <code>False</code> on <code>ValueError</code> (allow <code>&quot;&quot;</code>, <code>&quot;-&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;.&quot;</code> too, so the user can start typing).</p>","see":["tk.entry-number"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() def only_digits (new_text): return new_text == \"\" or new_text.isdigit() def max_five (new_text): return len (new_text) <= 5 vd = root.register(only_digits) vl = root.register(max_five) tk.Label(root, text= \"Digits only\" ).pack() tk.Entry(root, validate= \"key\" , validatecommand=(vd, \"%P\" )).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Label(root, text= \"Max 5 characters\" ).pack() tk.Entry(root, validate= \"key\" , validatecommand=(vl, \"%P\" )).pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() For decimals use a test like: try float(new_text) and return False on ValueError (allow \"\" , \"-\" and \".\" too, so the user can start typing)."},{"id":"tk.entry-readonly","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make an Entry read-only (can select and copy but not type)?","qh":"How do I make an Entry read-only (can select and copy but not type)?","a":"<code>entry.config(state=&quot;readonly&quot;)</code>. <code>&quot;disabled&quot;</code> also blocks selecting. Switch to <code>&quot;normal&quot;</code> before changing the text from code.","aka":"readonly entry, non editable entry, disable typing, entry state readonly, lock entry","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ne = tk.Entry(root)\ne.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;You can copy me&quot;</i>)\ne.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() e = tk.Entry(root) e.insert( 0 , \"You can copy me\" ) e.config(state= \"readonly\" ) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-focus","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I put the cursor in an Entry automatically (focus)?","qh":"How do I put the cursor in an Entry automatically (focus)?","a":"Call <code>entry.focus()</code> (same as <code>focus_set()</code>). Tab moves to the next widget in the order they were created.","aka":"focus entry, autofocus, focus_set, cursor in textbox at start, move to next entry, tab order","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nfirst = tk.Entry(root); first.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nsecond = tk.Entry(root); second.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nfirst.focus()\nfirst.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: second.focus())   <i class=\"c\"># Enter jumps to the next box</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Change the Tab order with <code>widget.lift()</code> or by creating widgets in the order you want.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() first = tk.Entry(root); first.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) second = tk.Entry(root); second.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) first.focus() first.bind( \"<Return>\" , lambda e: second.focus()) # Enter jumps to the next box root.mainloop() Change the Tab order with widget.lift() or by creating widgets in the order you want."},{"id":"tk.entry-width","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make an Entry wider or taller?","qh":"How do I make an Entry wider or taller?","a":"<code>width=</code> is in characters. Entry is always one line; it gets taller with a bigger <code>font</code>. For several lines, use a <a href=\"#/a/tk.text\">Text</a> widget.","aka":"entry width, entry height, bigger textbox, large input, entry font size","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root, width= 40 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root, font=( \"Arial\" , 18 )).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.entry-changes","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I run code every time the Entry text changes?","qh":"How do I run code every time the Entry text changes?","a":"Give the Entry a <code>StringVar</code> and use <code>var.trace_add(&quot;write&quot;, callback)</code>.","aka":"entry on change, text changed event, live search, trace entry, detect typing, onchange","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntext = tk.StringVar()\ntk.Entry(root, textvariable=text).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ncount = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0 characters&quot;</i>)\ncount.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">changed</i>(*args):\n    count.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(text.get())}<i class=\"s\"> characters</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\ntext.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, changed)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.trace"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() text = tk.StringVar() tk.Entry(root, textvariable=text).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) count = tk.Label(root, text= \"0 characters\" ) count.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def changed (*args): count.config(text= f\" { len (text.get())} characters \" ) text.trace_add( \"write\" , changed) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make a multi-line text box?","qh":"How do I make a multi-line text box?","a":"Use <code>tk.Text(root, width=40, height=10)</code>. Unlike Entry, its positions are <code>&quot;line.column&quot;</code> strings: start is <code>&quot;1.0&quot;</code>, end is <code>&quot;end&quot;</code>.","aka":"text widget, textarea, multiline input, big text box, notes box, text editor box, tk.Text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;word&quot;</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Type anything here.\\nIt can have many lines.&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>wrap=&quot;word&quot;</code> wraps at spaces; <code>&quot;char&quot;</code> (default) can split words; <code>&quot;none&quot;</code> doesn't wrap.</p>","see":["tk.text-get","tk.text-insert","tk.text-scroll"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() box = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 8 , wrap= \"word\" ) box.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) box.insert( \"1.0\" , \"Type anything here.\\nIt can have many lines.\" ) root.mainloop() wrap=\"word\" wraps at spaces; \"char\" (default) can split words; \"none\" doesn't wrap."},{"id":"tk.text-get","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get all the text from a Text widget?","qh":"How do I get all the text from a Text widget?","a":"<code>text.get(&quot;1.0&quot;, &quot;end-1c&quot;)</code>. <code>&quot;1.0&quot;</code> = line 1, character 0. <code>&quot;end-1c&quot;</code> drops the newline that Tk always adds at the end.","aka":"text get, read text widget, get contents of textarea, text get 1.0 end, get text box value","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;line one\\nline two&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    content = box.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(content))\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;lines:&quot;</i>, content.count(<i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Get&quot;</i>, command=show).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() box = tk.Text(root, width= 30 , height= 5 ) box.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) box.insert( \"1.0\" , \"line one\\nline two\" ) def show (): content = box.get( \"1.0\" , \"end-1c\" ) print ( repr (content)) print ( \"lines:\" , content.count( \"\\n\" ) + 1 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Get\" , command=show).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-insert","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add, replace or clear text in a Text widget?","qh":"How do I add, replace or clear text in a Text widget?","a":"<code>insert(&quot;end&quot;, &quot;more\\n&quot;)</code> appends, <code>delete(&quot;1.0&quot;, &quot;end&quot;)</code> clears everything. Call <code>see(&quot;end&quot;)</code> to scroll to the newest line.","aka":"text insert, text delete, clear text widget, append text, replace text, write to text box, log window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlog = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nlog.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nn = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add_line</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> n\n    n += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    log.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Message </i>{n}<i class=\"s\">\\n</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    log.see(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Add&quot;</i>, command=add_line).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: log.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.text-readonly"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() log = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 ) log.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) n = 0 def add_line (): global n n += 1 log.insert( \"end\" , f\" Message {n} \\n \" ) log.see( \"end\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Add\" , command=add_line).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : log.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-readonly","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I make a Text widget read-only?","qh":"How do I make a Text widget read-only?","a":"<code>text.config(state=&quot;disabled&quot;)</code>. To change it from code, switch to <code>&quot;normal&quot;</code>, edit, then back to <code>&quot;disabled&quot;</code>.","aka":"read only text, non editable text box, disable text widget, output box, stop typing in text","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nout = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">36</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\nout.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">write</i>(msg):\n    out.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;normal&quot;</i>)\n    out.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, msg + <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>)\n    out.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n    out.see(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Write&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: write(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Read-only output&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() out = tk.Text(root, width= 36 , height= 5 , state= \"disabled\" ) out.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def write (msg): out.config(state= \"normal\" ) out.insert( \"end\" , msg + \"\\n\" ) out.config(state= \"disabled\" ) out.see( \"end\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Write\" , command= lambda : write( \"Read-only output\" )).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-scroll","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Text widget?","qh":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Text widget?","a":"Easiest: <code>from tkinter import scrolledtext</code> and use <code>scrolledtext.ScrolledText</code>. Or connect a <code>Scrollbar</code> by hand.","aka":"scroll text, scrollbar text box, scrolledtext, scrolling textarea","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> scrolledtext\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = scrolledtext.ScrolledText(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;word&quot;</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nbox.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>.join(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Line </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">51</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scrollbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import scrolledtext root = tk.Tk() box = scrolledtext.ScrolledText(root, width= 40 , height= 8 , wrap= \"word\" ) box.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) box.insert( \"1.0\" , \"\\n\" .join( f\" Line {i} \" for i in range ( 1 , 51 ))) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-tags","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I colour or style part of the text in a Text widget?","qh":"How do I colour or style part of the text in a Text widget?","a":"Create a tag with <code>tag_configure(&quot;name&quot;, foreground=..., font=...)</code>, then use it when inserting or with <code>tag_add(name, start, end)</code>.","aka":"text tag, highlight text, colored text, bold part of text, tag_configure, syntax highlight, change color of word","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">12</i>))\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, foreground=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;big&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>))\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hl&quot;</i>, background=<i class=\"s\">&quot;yellow&quot;</i>)\n\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Normal &quot;</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red &quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;big\\n&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;big&quot;</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;highlight this word&quot;</i>)\nt.tag_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;hl&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.10&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.14&quot;</i>)    <i class=\"c\"># line 2, characters 10 to 14</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.text-search"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 , font=( \"Courier\" , 12 )) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) t.tag_configure( \"red\" , foreground= \"red\" ) t.tag_configure( \"big\" , font=( \"Courier\" , 16 , \"bold\" )) t.tag_configure( \"hl\" , background= \"yellow\" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"Normal \" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"red \" , \"red\" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"big\\n\" , \"big\" ) t.insert( \"end\" , \"highlight this word\" ) t.tag_add( \"hl\" , \"2.10\" , \"2.14\" ) # line 2, characters 10 to 14 root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-search","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I find and highlight a word in a Text widget?","qh":"How do I find and highlight a word in a Text widget?","a":"Loop with <code>text.search(word, start, stopindex=&quot;end&quot;)</code>; it returns the index of the next match or <code>&quot;&quot;</code>.","aka":"search text, find in text, find all occurrences, highlight search results, text search","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;the cat sat on the mat with the hat&quot;</i>)\nt.tag_configure(<i class=\"s\">&quot;found&quot;</i>, background=<i class=\"s\">&quot;yellow&quot;</i>)\nq = tk.Entry(root); q.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)); q.insert(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;the&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">find</i>():\n    t.tag_remove(<i class=\"s\">&quot;found&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    word = q.get()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> word:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    pos = <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n        pos = t.search(word, pos, stopindex=<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, nocase=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">if</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> pos:\n            <i class=\"k\">break</i>\n        end = <i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{pos}<i class=\"s\">+</i>{<i class=\"b\">len</i>(word)}<i class=\"s\">c</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>\n        t.tag_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;found&quot;</i>, pos, end)\n        pos = end\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Find&quot;</i>, command=find).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.text-tags"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 5 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"the cat sat on the mat with the hat\" ) t.tag_configure( \"found\" , background= \"yellow\" ) q = tk.Entry(root); q.pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady=( 0 , 10 )); q.insert( 0 , \"the\" ) def find (): t.tag_remove( \"found\" , \"1.0\" , \"end\" ) word = q.get() if not word: return pos = \"1.0\" while True : pos = t.search(word, pos, stopindex= \"end\" , nocase= True ) if not pos: break end = f\" {pos} + { len (word)} c \" t.tag_add( \"found\" , pos, end) pos = end tk.Button(root, text= \"Find\" , command=find).pack(side= \"left\" , pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-undo","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I add undo/redo to a Text widget?","qh":"How do I add undo/redo to a Text widget?","a":"Create it with <code>undo=True</code>. Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y (Cmd+Z / Cmd+Shift+Z on Mac) then work, and you can call <code>edit_undo()</code> / <code>edit_redo()</code>.","aka":"undo text, ctrl z text widget, redo, edit_undo","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, undo=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, maxundo=-<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">undo</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        t.edit_undo()\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>      <i class=\"c\"># nothing to undo</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Undo&quot;</i>, command=undo).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 40 , height= 6 , undo= True , maxundo=- 1 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def undo (): try : t.edit_undo() except tk.TclError: pass # nothing to undo tk.Button(root, text= \"Undo\" , command=undo).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-cursor","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"How do I get or move the cursor position in a Text or Entry?","qh":"How do I get or move the cursor position in a Text or Entry?","a":"The cursor is the <code>&quot;insert&quot;</code> index. Read it with <code>text.index(&quot;insert&quot;)</code>; move it with <code>text.mark_set(&quot;insert&quot;, &quot;1.0&quot;)</code>. For an Entry use <code>entry.index(&quot;insert&quot;)</code> and <code>entry.icursor(n)</code>.","aka":"insert cursor, cursor position, caret, current line number, move cursor end, index insert","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;hello\\nworld&quot;</i>)\npos = tk.Label(root)\npos.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">update</i>(e=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    line, col = t.index(<i class=\"s\">&quot;insert&quot;</i>).split(<i class=\"s\">&quot;.&quot;</i>)\n    pos.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Line </i>{line}<i class=\"s\">, column </i>{<i class=\"b\">int</i>(col) + <i class=\"n\">1</i>}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;KeyRelease&gt;&quot;</i>, update)\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;ButtonRelease-1&gt;&quot;</i>, update)\nt.mark_set(<i class=\"s\">&quot;insert&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\nupdate()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 30 , height= 4 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"hello\\nworld\" ) pos = tk.Label(root) pos.pack() def update (e= None ): line, col = t.index( \"insert\" ).split( \".\" ) pos.config(text= f\" Line {line} , column { int (col) + 1 } \" ) t.bind( \"<KeyRelease>\" , update) t.bind( \"<ButtonRelease-1>\" , update) t.mark_set( \"insert\" , \"end\" ) update() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.text-indexes","s":"tk-entry-text","q":"What do Text indexes like \"1.0\", \"end-1c\", \"insert lineend\" mean?","qh":"What do Text indexes like \"1.0\", \"end-1c\", \"insert lineend\" mean?","a":"<code>&quot;line.column&quot;</code>: lines start at 1, columns at 0. You can add modifiers: <code>+5c</code> (characters), <code>-1l</code> (lines), <code>linestart</code>, <code>lineend</code>, <code>wordstart</code>, <code>wordend</code>.","aka":"text index, 1.0, end-1c, linestart, lineend, wordstart, text positions","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;first line\\nsecond line\\n&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.end&quot;</i>))              <i class=\"c\"># first line</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;2.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.6&quot;</i>))                <i class=\"c\"># second</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0 + 5c&quot;</i>))           <i class=\"c\"># first</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;2.3 wordstart&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;2.3 wordend&quot;</i>))   <i class=\"c\"># second</i>\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(t.index(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>))                  <i class=\"c\"># 3.0 (very last position)</i>\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"first line\\nsecond line\\n\" ) print (t.get( \"1.0\" , \"1.end\" )) # first line print (t.get( \"2.0\" , \"2.6\" )) # second print (t.get( \"1.0\" , \"1.0 + 5c\" )) # first print (t.get( \"2.3 wordstart\" , \"2.3 wordend\" )) # second print (t.index( \"end-1c\" )) # 3.0 (very last position) root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.checkbutton","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a checkbox and read whether it's ticked?","qh":"How do I make a checkbox and read whether it's ticked?","a":"Link it to a <code>BooleanVar</code> (or <code>IntVar</code>) with <code>variable=</code>, then read <code>var.get()</code>.","aka":"checkbox, checkbutton, tick box, check box value, is checked, BooleanVar checkbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsugar = tk.BooleanVar(value=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sugar&quot;</i>, variable=sugar).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">order</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sugar:&quot;</i>, sugar.get())     <i class=\"c\"># True or False</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Order&quot;</i>, command=order).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>command=</code> runs a function every time it's clicked. With <code>IntVar</code>, it gives 1/0; set <code>onvalue</code>/<code>offvalue</code> for other values.</p>","see":["tk.checkbutton-many","tk.booleanvar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() sugar = tk.BooleanVar(value= True ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Sugar\" , variable=sugar).pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def order (): print ( \"Sugar:\" , sugar.get()) # True or False tk.Button(root, text= \"Order\" , command=order).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() command= runs a function every time it's clicked. With IntVar , it gives 1/0; set onvalue / offvalue for other values."},{"id":"tk.checkbutton-many","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make many checkboxes from a list and see which are ticked?","qh":"How do I make many checkboxes from a list and see which are ticked?","a":"Keep a dict of <code>{name: BooleanVar}</code> and loop over it.","aka":"multiple checkboxes, list of checkbuttons, checkboxes in loop, get selected checkboxes","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntoppings = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Cheese&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Ham&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Pineapple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Olives&quot;</i>]\nchosen = {}\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> toppings:\n    chosen[t] = tk.BooleanVar()\n    tk.Checkbutton(root, text=t, variable=chosen[t]).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    picked = [t <i class=\"k\">for</i> t, v <i class=\"k\">in</i> chosen.items() <i class=\"k\">if</i> v.get()]\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Picked:&quot;</i>, picked)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Done&quot;</i>, command=show).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() toppings = [ \"Cheese\" , \"Ham\" , \"Pineapple\" , \"Olives\" ] chosen = {} for t in toppings: chosen[t] = tk.BooleanVar() tk.Checkbutton(root, text=t, variable=chosen[t]).pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 20 ) def show (): picked = [t for t, v in chosen.items() if v.get()] print ( \"Picked:\" , picked) tk.Button(root, text= \"Done\" , command=show).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.radiobutton","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make radio buttons (choose one option)?","qh":"How do I make radio buttons (choose one option)?","a":"Give every Radiobutton the <b>same</b> variable and a different <code>value=</code>. The variable holds the chosen value.","aka":"radio button, option buttons, choose one, multiple choice, radiobutton value, quiz options","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nsize = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> text, val <i class=\"k\">in</i> [(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Small&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;S&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Medium&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>), (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Large&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;L&quot;</i>)]:\n    tk.Radiobutton(root, text=text, variable=size, value=val).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OK&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Size:&quot;</i>, size.get())).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>If all of them start selected (or none can be picked), they don't share one variable, or the variable was garbage-collected (it was created inside a function and not stored).</p>","see":["tk.recipe-quiz"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() size = tk.StringVar(value= \"M\" ) for text, val in [( \"Small\" , \"S\" ), ( \"Medium\" , \"M\" ), ( \"Large\" , \"L\" )]: tk.Radiobutton(root, text=text, variable=size, value=val).pack(anchor= \"w\" , padx= 20 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"OK\" , command= lambda : print ( \"Size:\" , size.get())).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() If all of them start selected (or none can be picked), they don't share one variable, or the variable was garbage-collected (it was created inside a function and not stored)."},{"id":"tk.combobox","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a dropdown menu (combobox)?","qh":"How do I make a dropdown menu (combobox)?","a":"Use <code>ttk.Combobox(root, values=[...], state=&quot;readonly&quot;)</code> and read it with <code>.get()</code>.","aka":"dropdown, drop down list, combobox, select box, choose from list, ttk.Combobox, picker","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nfruit = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Banana&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Cherry&quot;</i>], state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nfruit.current(<i class=\"n\">0</i>)             <i class=\"c\"># select the first item</i>\nfruit.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Show&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(fruit.get())).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Without <code>state=&quot;readonly&quot;</code> the user can also type their own value.</p>","see":["tk.combobox-event","tk.optionmenu"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() fruit = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[ \"Apple\" , \"Banana\" , \"Cherry\" ], state= \"readonly\" ) fruit.current( 0 ) # select the first item fruit.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Show\" , command= lambda : print (fruit.get())).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() Without state=\"readonly\" the user can also type their own value."},{"id":"tk.combobox-event","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I run code when the user picks something from a Combobox?","qh":"How do I run code when the user picks something from a Combobox?","a":"Bind the virtual event <code>&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</code>.","aka":"combobox selected event, ComboboxSelected, on select dropdown, combobox change, combobox callback","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nbox = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Red&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Green&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Blue&quot;</i>], state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nbox.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nswatch = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;     &quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nswatch.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">picked</i>(event):\n    swatch.config(bg=box.get().lower())\n\nbox.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, picked)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.combobox","tk.combobox-update"],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() box = ttk.Combobox(root, values=[ \"Red\" , \"Green\" , \"Blue\" ], state= \"readonly\" ) box.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) swatch = tk.Label(root, text= \" \" , width= 20 ) swatch.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def picked (event): swatch.config(bg=box.get().lower()) box.bind( \"<<ComboboxSelected>>\" , picked) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.combobox-update","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I change the list of options in a Combobox later (dependent dropdowns)?","qh":"How do I change the list of options in a Combobox later (dependent dropdowns)?","a":"<code>box[&quot;values&quot;] = new_list</code> (or <code>box.config(values=...)</code>), then <code>box.set(&quot;&quot;)</code> to clear the old choice.","aka":"update combobox values, dynamic dropdown, second dropdown depends on first, combobox set values","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\ndata = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Fruit&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Apple&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Pear&quot;</i>], <i class=\"s\">&quot;Veg&quot;</i>: [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Carrot&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Leek&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Pea&quot;</i>]}\nroot = tk.Tk()\nkind = ttk.Combobox(root, values=<i class=\"b\">list</i>(data), state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nitem = ttk.Combobox(root, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nkind.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nitem.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">kind_changed</i>(e):\n    item[<i class=\"s\">&quot;values&quot;</i>] = data[kind.get()]\n    item.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\n\nkind.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, kind_changed)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk data = { \"Fruit\" : [ \"Apple\" , \"Pear\" ], \"Veg\" : [ \"Carrot\" , \"Leek\" , \"Pea\" ]} root = tk.Tk() kind = ttk.Combobox(root, values= list (data), state= \"readonly\" ) item = ttk.Combobox(root, state= \"readonly\" ) kind.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) item.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) def kind_changed (e): item[ \"values\" ] = data[kind.get()] item. set ( \"\" ) kind.bind( \"<<ComboboxSelected>>\" , kind_changed) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.optionmenu","s":"tk-choices","q":"What is OptionMenu and how do I use it?","qh":"What is OptionMenu and how do I use it?","a":"<code>tk.OptionMenu(root, var, *choices)</code> is a simple dropdown button. Note the <code>*</code> before the list.","aka":"optionmenu, tk.OptionMenu, menu button dropdown, simple dropdown","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nchoices = [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Easy&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Normal&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Hard&quot;</i>]\nlevel = tk.StringVar(value=choices[<i class=\"n\">1</i>])\ntk.OptionMenu(root, level, *choices).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nlevel.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> *a: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Level:&quot;</i>, level.get()))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To change its choices later:</p><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nvar = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)\nom = tk.OptionMenu(root, var, <i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;b&quot;</i>)\nom.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">set_options</i>(options):\n    menu = om[<i class=\"s\">&quot;menu&quot;</i>]\n    menu.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> o <i class=\"k\">in</i> options:\n        menu.add_command(label=o, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i> v=o: var.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(v))\n    var.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(options[<i class=\"n\">0</i>])\n\nset_options([<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;z&quot;</i>])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.combobox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() choices = [ \"Easy\" , \"Normal\" , \"Hard\" ] level = tk.StringVar(value=choices[ 1 ]) tk.OptionMenu(root, level, *choices).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) level.trace_add( \"write\" , lambda *a: print ( \"Level:\" , level.get())) root.mainloop() To change its choices later: import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() var = tk.StringVar(value= \"a\" ) om = tk.OptionMenu(root, var, \"a\" , \"b\" ) om.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def set_options (options): menu = om[ \"menu\" ] menu.delete( 0 , \"end\" ) for o in options: menu.add_command(label=o, command= lambda v=o: var. set (v)) var. set (options[ 0 ]) set_options([ \"x\" , \"y\" , \"z\" ]) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.listbox","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a list the user can pick from (Listbox)?","qh":"How do I make a list the user can pick from (Listbox)?","a":"Create <code>tk.Listbox</code>, add items with <code>insert(&quot;end&quot;, item)</code>, and read the selection with <code>curselection()</code>.","aka":"listbox, list widget, list of items, show list, selectable list, tk.Listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, height=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> name <i class=\"k\">in</i> [<i class=\"s\">&quot;Ada&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Bob&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Cara&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Dev&quot;</i>]:\n    lb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, name)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>():\n    sel = lb.curselection()          <i class=\"c\"># a tuple of indexes, e.g. (2,)</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> sel:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Picked:&quot;</i>, lb.get(sel[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]))\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Show&quot;</i>, command=show).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To fill it from a list in one go: <code>lb.insert(&quot;end&quot;, *my_list)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.listbox-select","tk.listbox-delete","tk.listbox-scroll"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, height= 6 ) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) for name in [ \"Ada\" , \"Bob\" , \"Cara\" , \"Dev\" ]: lb.insert( \"end\" , name) def show (): sel = lb.curselection() # a tuple of indexes, e.g. (2,) if sel: print ( \"Picked:\" , lb.get(sel[ 0 ])) tk.Button(root, text= \"Show\" , command=show).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() To fill it from a list in one go: lb.insert(\"end\", *my_list) ."},{"id":"tk.listbox-select","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I run code when a Listbox item is clicked or double-clicked?","qh":"How do I run code when a Listbox item is clicked or double-clicked?","a":"Bind <code>&quot;&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</code> for single selection changes, <code>&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</code> for double-click.","aka":"listbox select event, ListboxSelect, double click listbox, on select listbox, listbox click","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Paris&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tokyo&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Lima&quot;</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ninfo = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\ninfo.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">selected</i>(e):\n    sel = lb.curselection()\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> sel:\n        info.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Selected &quot;</i> + lb.get(sel[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]))\n\nlb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, selected)\nlb.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Opened&quot;</i>, lb.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;active&quot;</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>&lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt;</code> also fires when the selection is cleared, so check <code>curselection()</code> isn't empty.</p>","see":["tk.listbox"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root) lb.insert( \"end\" , \"Paris\" , \"Tokyo\" , \"Lima\" ) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) info = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) info.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) def selected (e): sel = lb.curselection() if sel: info.config(text= \"Selected \" + lb.get(sel[ 0 ])) lb.bind( \"<<ListboxSelect>>\" , selected) lb.bind( \"<Double-Button-1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"Opened\" , lb.get( \"active\" ))) root.mainloop() <<ListboxSelect>> also fires when the selection is cleared, so check curselection() isn't empty."},{"id":"tk.listbox-delete","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I delete items from a Listbox (selected or all)?","qh":"How do I delete items from a Listbox (selected or all)?","a":"<code>lb.delete(index)</code> for one item, <code>lb.delete(0, &quot;end&quot;)</code> for all. For the selection, delete in <b>reverse</b> order so the indexes don't shift.","aka":"listbox delete, remove selected item, clear listbox, delete all items listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;extended&quot;</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, *[<i class=\"s\">&quot;one&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;two&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;three&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;four&quot;</i>])\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">remove_selected</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">reversed</i>(lb.curselection()):\n        lb.delete(i)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Delete selected&quot;</i>, command=remove_selected).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clear&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: lb.delete(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode= \"extended\" ) lb.insert( \"end\" , *[ \"one\" , \"two\" , \"three\" , \"four\" ]) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def remove_selected (): for i in reversed (lb.curselection()): lb.delete(i) tk.Button(root, text= \"Delete selected\" , command=remove_selected).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Clear\" , command= lambda : lb.delete( 0 , \"end\" )).pack(side= \"left\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.listbox-multi","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I let the user select several items in a Listbox?","qh":"How do I let the user select several items in a Listbox?","a":"<code>selectmode=&quot;multiple&quot;</code> (click toggles) or <code>&quot;extended&quot;</code> (Shift/Ctrl like a file manager). <code>curselection()</code> gives all indexes.","aka":"multiple selection, selectmode, multi select listbox, select many","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode=<i class=\"s\">&quot;multiple&quot;</i>, exportselection=<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, *[<i class=\"s\">&quot;Mon&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Tue&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Wed&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Thu&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Fri&quot;</i>])\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Days&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>([lb.get(i) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> lb.curselection()])).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>exportselection=False</code> stops the selection vanishing when you select text somewhere else (common with two listboxes).</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root, selectmode= \"multiple\" , exportselection= False ) lb.insert( \"end\" , *[ \"Mon\" , \"Tue\" , \"Wed\" , \"Thu\" , \"Fri\" ]) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Days\" , command= lambda : print ([lb.get(i) for i in lb.curselection()])).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() exportselection=False stops the selection vanishing when you select text somewhere else (common with two listboxes)."},{"id":"tk.listbox-scroll","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Listbox?","qh":"How do I add a scrollbar to a Listbox?","a":"Put both in a Frame, then connect them: <code>yscrollcommand=sb.set</code> on the listbox and <code>command=lb.yview</code> on the scrollbar.","aka":"listbox scrollbar, scroll list, long listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nframe = tk.Frame(root)\nframe.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nsb = tk.Scrollbar(frame, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>)\nlb = tk.Listbox(frame, yscrollcommand=sb.<i class=\"b\">set</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nsb.config(command=lb.yview)\nsb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>)\nlb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, *[<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Item </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">100</i>)])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scrollbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() frame = tk.Frame(root) frame.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) sb = tk.Scrollbar(frame, orient= \"vertical\" ) lb = tk.Listbox(frame, yscrollcommand=sb. set , height= 8 ) sb.config(command=lb.yview) sb.pack(side= \"right\" , fill= \"y\" ) lb.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) lb.insert( \"end\" , *[ f\" Item {i} \" for i in range ( 100 )]) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.listbox-colors","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I change the colour of one Listbox item or edit an item?","qh":"How do I change the colour of one Listbox item or edit an item?","a":"Colour: <code>lb.itemconfig(i, fg=&quot;red&quot;, bg=&quot;#fee&quot;)</code>. Edit: there's no \"replace\", so <code>delete(i)</code> then <code>insert(i, new)</code>.","aka":"listbox item color, itemconfig, update listbox item, edit item listbox","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlb = tk.Listbox(root)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;done&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;late&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;todo&quot;</i>)\nlb.itemconfig(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>)\nlb.delete(<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\nlb.insert(<i class=\"n\">2</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;todo (edited)&quot;</i>)\nlb.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lb = tk.Listbox(root) lb.insert( \"end\" , \"done\" , \"late\" , \"todo\" ) lb.itemconfig( 1 , fg= \"white\" , bg= \"#c23b22\" ) lb.delete( 2 ) lb.insert( 2 , \"todo (edited)\" ) lb.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.scale","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a slider?","qh":"How do I make a slider?","a":"<code>tk.Scale(root, from_=0, to=100, orient=&quot;horizontal&quot;)</code>. Read it with <code>.get()</code> or a variable; <code>command=</code> runs while it moves.","aka":"slider, scale, range input, volume slider, ttk.Scale, horizontal slider, value slider","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nvol = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">50</i>)\ntk.Scale(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">100</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, variable=vol, length=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Volume&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=vol).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>It's <code>from_</code> with an underscore, because <code>from</code> is a Python keyword. For steps use <code>resolution=0.5</code> or <code>tickinterval=10</code>.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() vol = tk.IntVar(value= 50 ) tk.Scale(root, from_= 0 , to= 100 , orient= \"horizontal\" , variable=vol, length= 200 , label= \"Volume\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=vol).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() It's from_ with an underscore, because from is a Python keyword. For steps use resolution=0.5 or tickinterval=10 ."},{"id":"tk.spinbox","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a number box with up/down arrows (Spinbox)?","qh":"How do I make a number box with up/down arrows (Spinbox)?","a":"<code>tk.Spinbox(root, from_=1, to=10)</code> or with a list: <code>values=(&quot;S&quot;, &quot;M&quot;, &quot;L&quot;)</code>. Read with <code>.get()</code> (a string).","aka":"spinbox, number picker, up down arrows, increment box, numeric input","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nqty = tk.Spinbox(root, from_=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nqty.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nsize = tk.Spinbox(root, values=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;S&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;M&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;L&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;XL&quot;</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;readonly&quot;</i>)\nsize.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Buy&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(qty.get()), size.get())).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() qty = tk.Spinbox(root, from_= 1 , to= 10 , width= 5 ) qty.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) size = tk.Spinbox(root, values=( \"S\" , \"M\" , \"L\" , \"XL\" ), width= 5 , state= \"readonly\" ) size.pack(padx= 20 , pady=( 0 , 10 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Buy\" , command= lambda : print ( int (qty.get()), size.get())).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.checkbutton-switch","s":"tk-choices","q":"How do I make a toggle button (on/off switch)?","qh":"How do I make a toggle button (on/off switch)?","a":"A Checkbutton with <code>indicatoron=False</code> looks like a button that stays pressed. Or change a Button's text in its command.","aka":"toggle button, on off button, switch, indicatoron, push button toggle","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\non = tk.BooleanVar()\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Bold&quot;</i>, variable=on, indicatoron=<i class=\"k\">False</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, selectcolor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffcc66&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\nstate = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">False</i>}\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">flip</i>():\n    state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>] = <i class=\"k\">not</i> state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>]\n    sw.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ON&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;OFF&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#2e7d32&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> state[<i class=\"s\">&quot;on&quot;</i>] <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;#888&quot;</i>)\n\nsw = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;OFF&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#888&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">8</i>, command=flip)\nsw.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() on = tk.BooleanVar() tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Bold\" , variable=on, indicatoron= False , width= 8 , selectcolor= \"#ffcc66\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) state = { \"on\" : False } def flip (): state[ \"on\" ] = not state[ \"on\" ] sw.config(text= \"ON\" if state[ \"on\" ] else \"OFF\" , bg= \"#2e7d32\" if state[ \"on\" ] else \"#888\" ) sw = tk.Button(root, text= \"OFF\" , bg= \"#888\" , fg= \"white\" , width= 8 , command=flip) sw.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.layout-which","s":"tk-layout","q":"pack, grid or place: which should I use?","qh":"pack, grid or place: which should I use?","a":"<b>grid</b> for forms and anything in rows/columns, <b>pack</b> for simple stacks (top-to-bottom or side by side), <b>place</b> only for exact pixel positions. Group widgets in Frames and you can use a different one inside each Frame.","aka":"geometry manager, layout manager, pack vs grid, how to position widgets, arrange widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntop = tk.Frame(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>); top.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># pack in root</i>\ntk.Label(top, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Toolbar&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ddd&quot;</i>).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\n\nform = tk.Frame(root); form.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)            <i class=\"c\"># grid inside this frame</i>\ntk.Label(form, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(form).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\ntk.Label(form, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Email&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(form).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pack","tk.grid","tk.place","tk.err.pack-grid"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() top = tk.Frame(root, bg= \"#ddd\" ); top.pack(fill= \"x\" ) # pack in root tk.Label(top, text= \"Toolbar\" , bg= \"#ddd\" ).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 6 ) form = tk.Frame(root); form.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) # grid inside this frame tk.Label(form, text= \"Name\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" ) tk.Entry(form).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 ) tk.Label(form, text= \"Email\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" ) tk.Entry(form).grid(row= 1 , column= 1 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.pack","s":"tk-layout","q":"How does pack() work? How do I put widgets side by side?","qh":"How does pack() work? How do I put widgets side by side?","a":"Each <code>pack()</code> takes space from the remaining area, from the <code>side</code> you say (default <code>&quot;top&quot;</code>). Use <code>side=&quot;left&quot;</code> to place widgets in a row.","aka":"pack side left, widgets next to each other, horizontal layout, stack widgets, pack order","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;one&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;two&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;three&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Button(root, text=t).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Mixing sides in one container gets confusing fast. Put each row in its own Frame instead.</p>","see":["tk.pack-fill","tk.padding"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for t in ( \"one\" , \"two\" , \"three\" ): tk.Button(root, text=t).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 4 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Mixing sides in one container gets confusing fast. Put each row in its own Frame instead."},{"id":"tk.pack-fill","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a widget fill the window or stretch when it's resized?","qh":"How do I make a widget fill the window or stretch when it's resized?","a":"With pack: <code>fill=&quot;x&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;y&quot;</code>/<code>&quot;both&quot;</code> stretches it across its slot; <code>expand=True</code> makes its slot grow with the window. Usually you want both: <code>pack(fill=&quot;both&quot;, expand=True)</code>.","aka":"fill expand, stretch widget, fill both, expand true, widget resize with window, full width","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x220&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;fill x&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>)\ntk.Text(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">6</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;status bar&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ccc&quot;</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;bottom&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x220\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"fill x\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" ) tk.Text(root, bg= \"#eee\" ).pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True , padx= 6 , pady= 6 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"status bar\" , bg= \"#ccc\" , anchor= \"w\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , side= \"bottom\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid","s":"tk-layout","q":"How does grid() work?","qh":"How does grid() work?","a":"<code>widget.grid(row=r, column=c)</code>. Rows and columns start at 0, are only as big as their widest widget, and empty rows/columns take no space.","aka":"grid row column, table layout, form layout, rows and columns, grid tutorial","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Username&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Password&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, show=<i class=\"s\">&quot;*&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Log in&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Skipping to <code>column=10</code> won't leave a gap: empty columns have no width. Use <code>padx</code> or <code>minsize</code> for gaps.</p>","see":["tk.grid-sticky","tk.grid-span","tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Username\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Password\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Entry(root, show= \"*\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 1 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Log in\" ).grid(row= 2 , column= 1 , sticky= \"e\" , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop() Skipping to column=10 won't leave a gap: empty columns have no width. Use padx or minsize for gaps."},{"id":"tk.grid-sticky","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I align or stretch a widget inside its grid cell (sticky)?","qh":"How do I align or stretch a widget inside its grid cell (sticky)?","a":"<code>sticky</code> uses compass letters: <code>&quot;w&quot;</code> left, <code>&quot;e&quot;</code> right, <code>&quot;n&quot;</code> top, <code>&quot;s&quot;</code> bottom. <code>&quot;ew&quot;</code> stretches across, <code>&quot;nsew&quot;</code> fills the cell.","aka":"sticky, grid align left, grid stretch, nsew, sticky w e, align in grid cell","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a long label in column 0&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;e&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">3</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"a long label in column 0\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"w\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"e\" ).grid(row= 2 , column= 0 , sticky= \"e\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"ew\" ).grid(row= 3 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid-span","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a widget span several columns or rows?","qh":"How do I make a widget span several columns or rows?","a":"<code>grid(row=2, column=0, columnspan=2)</code>; same with <code>rowspan</code>. Add <code>sticky=&quot;ew&quot;</code> to stretch it over the whole span.","aka":"columnspan, rowspan, merge cells, widget across two columns, full width button grid","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Wide button&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Wide button\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , sticky= \"ew\" , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid-weight","s":"tk-layout","q":"Why don't my grid widgets stretch when the window is resized?","qh":"Why don't my grid widgets stretch when the window is resized?","a":"Give the row/column a weight: <code>root.columnconfigure(0, weight=1)</code> and <code>root.rowconfigure(0, weight=1)</code>, <b>and</b> use <code>sticky=&quot;nsew&quot;</code> on the widget.","aka":"grid resize, columnconfigure weight, rowconfigure, grid expand, responsive grid, grid fill window","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;320x200&quot;</i>)\nroot.columnconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># column 0 takes extra width</i>\nroot.rowconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)           <i class=\"c\"># row 0 takes extra height</i>\ntk.Text(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, columnspan=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Send&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Weights share extra space: weight 2 gets twice as much as weight 1. <code>uniform=&quot;x&quot;</code> keeps columns the same size.</p>","see":["tk.grid-sticky","tk.pack-fill"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"320x200\" ) root.columnconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) # column 0 takes extra width root.rowconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) # row 0 takes extra height tk.Text(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , columnspan= 2 , sticky= \"nsew\" , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Send\" ).grid(row= 1 , column= 1 , padx= 5 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop() Weights share extra space: weight 2 gets twice as much as weight 1. uniform=\"x\" keeps columns the same size."},{"id":"tk.place","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I put a widget at an exact position (x, y)?","qh":"How do I put a widget at an exact position (x, y)?","a":"<code>widget.place(x=50, y=20)</code>. Relative: <code>place(relx=0.5, rely=0.5, anchor=&quot;center&quot;)</code> centres it whatever the window size.","aka":"place, absolute position, x y coordinates widget, relx rely, position widget pixel","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;at 10,10&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).place(x=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, y=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>).place(relx=<i class=\"n\">0.5</i>, rely=<i class=\"n\">0.5</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;center&quot;</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;bottom right&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ccc&quot;</i>).place(relx=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, rely=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;se&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>place</code> doesn't make the window grow to fit, so set a size with <code>geometry()</code>.</p>","see":["tk.center-widget"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"at 10,10\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).place(x= 10 , y= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"center\" ).place(relx= 0.5 , rely= 0.5 , anchor= \"center\" ) tk.Label(root, text= \"bottom right\" , bg= \"#ccc\" ).place(relx= 1 , rely= 1 , anchor= \"se\" ) root.mainloop() place doesn't make the window grow to fit, so set a size with geometry() ."},{"id":"tk.center-widget","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I centre a widget in the window?","qh":"How do I centre a widget in the window?","a":"Quickest: <code>place(relx=.5, rely=.5, anchor=&quot;center&quot;)</code>. With pack: <code>pack(expand=True)</code>. With grid: give the row and column weight and no sticky.","aka":"center button, centre label, middle of window, center widget, align center","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;I&#x27;m in the middle&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"I'm in the middle\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.padding","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I add space between widgets?","qh":"How do I add space between widgets?","a":"<code>padx</code>/<code>pady</code> in <code>pack()</code>/<code>grid()</code> add space <b>outside</b> the widget; give a pair for different sides: <code>pady=(10, 0)</code>. <code>ipadx</code>/<code>ipady</code> add space <b>inside</b> it.","aka":"padding, margin, padx pady, space between buttons, gap, ipadx, spacing","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;padx=20&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;pady=(20, 0): only above&quot;</i>).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ipadx=20 ipady=10: bigger inside&quot;</i>).pack(ipadx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, ipady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Button(root, text= \"padx=20\" ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"pady=(20, 0): only above\" ).pack(pady=( 20 , 0 )) tk.Button(root, text= \"ipadx=20 ipady=10: bigger inside\" ).pack(ipadx= 20 , ipady= 10 , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.err.pack-grid","s":"tk-layout","q":"TclError: cannot use geometry manager grid inside . which already has slaves managed by pack","qh":"TclError: cannot use geometry manager grid inside . which already has slaves managed by pack","a":"In one container (window or Frame) every child must use the <b>same</b> manager. Use <code>pack</code> for everything in <code>root</code>, or <code>grid</code> for everything; put a Frame in between if you need both.","aka":"cannot use geometry manager, mixing pack and grid, pack and grid together, already has slaves managed by, content managed by","err":"cannot use geometry manager*inside*which already has*managed by","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Title&quot;</i>).pack()\ntk.Entry(root).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)       <i class=\"c\"># TclError: root already uses pack</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Title&quot;</i>).pack()\nform = tk.Frame(root)\nform.pack()\ntk.Entry(form).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)        <i class=\"c\"># fine: grid is inside the frame</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>The message says <code>.</code> because that's Tk's name for the root window. A name like <code>.!frame</code> means a Frame.</p>","see":["tk.layout-which"],"t":"tk.Label(root, text= \"Title\" ).pack() tk.Entry(root).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ) # TclError: root already uses pack import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Title\" ).pack() form = tk.Frame(root) form.pack() tk.Entry(form).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 ) # fine: grid is inside the frame root.mainloop() The message says . because that's Tk's name for the root window. A name like .!frame means a Frame."},{"id":"tk.err.none-grid","s":"tk-layout","q":"AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...)","qh":"AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' (or 'config', 'insert'...)","a":"You did <code>x = tk.Entry(root).grid(...)</code>. <code>grid()</code>/<code>pack()</code>/<code>place()</code> return <code>None</code>, so <code>x</code> is <code>None</code>. Create the widget first, then lay it out on a <b>separate line</b>.","aka":"nonetype has no attribute get, entry get none, label config none, grid returns none, pack returns none","err":"'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'config'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'insert'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'delete'|'NoneType' object has no attribute 'configure'|'NoneType' object has no attribute","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre>name = tk.Entry(root).pack()\nname.get()          <i class=\"c\"># AttributeError: &#x27;NoneType&#x27; object has no attribute &#x27;get&#x27;</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.Entry(root)\nname.pack()\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"b\">repr</i>(name.get()))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Chaining is fine when you never need the widget again: <code>tk.Label(root, text=&quot;Hi&quot;).pack()</code>.</p>","see":["py.err.nonetype"],"t":"name = tk.Entry(root).pack() name.get() # AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'get' import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.Entry(root) name.pack() print ( repr (name.get())) root.mainloop() Chaining is fine when you never need the widget again: tk.Label(root, text=\"Hi\").pack() ."},{"id":"tk.frame","s":"tk-layout","q":"What is a Frame and how do I use it to group widgets?","qh":"What is a Frame and how do I use it to group widgets?","a":"A <code>Frame</code> is an invisible box that holds other widgets. Lay out the Frame in the window, then the widgets inside the Frame. <code>LabelFrame</code> adds a border with a title.","aka":"frame, container, group widgets, panel, sections in window, labelframe, border around widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nleft = tk.Frame(root, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nleft.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> t <i class=\"k\">in</i> (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Home&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Files&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;Settings&quot;</i>):\n    tk.Button(left, text=t, width=<i class=\"n\">10</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">2</i>)\n\nbox = tk.LabelFrame(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Details&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nbox.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\ntk.Label(box, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Name:&quot;</i>).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(box).grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>An empty Frame shrinks to nothing. To keep a set size: <code>Frame(root, width=200, height=100)</code> plus <code>frame.pack_propagate(False)</code> (or <code>grid_propagate(False)</code>).</p>","see":["tk.layout-which"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() left = tk.Frame(root, bg= \"#eee\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) left.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"y\" ) for t in ( \"Home\" , \"Files\" , \"Settings\" ): tk.Button(left, text=t, width= 10 ).pack(pady= 2 ) box = tk.LabelFrame(root, text= \"Details\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) box.pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) tk.Label(box, text= \"Name:\" ).grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"w\" ) tk.Entry(box).grid(row= 0 , column= 1 ) root.mainloop() An empty Frame shrinks to nothing. To keep a set size: Frame(root, width=200, height=100) plus frame.pack_propagate(False) (or grid_propagate(False) )."},{"id":"tk.frame-size","s":"tk-layout","q":"Why does my Frame ignore its width and height?","qh":"Why does my Frame ignore its width and height?","a":"A Frame shrinks or grows to fit its children. Turn that off with <code>frame.pack_propagate(False)</code> (if children use pack) or <code>grid_propagate(False)</code> (if they use grid).","aka":"frame size ignored, pack_propagate, grid_propagate, frame shrinks, fixed size frame","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nf = tk.Frame(root, width=<i class=\"n\">250</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">120</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>)\nf.pack_propagate(<i class=\"k\">False</i>)\nf.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(f, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;fixed 250x120 frame&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>).pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() f = tk.Frame(root, width= 250 , height= 120 , bg= \"#fc6\" ) f.pack_propagate( False ) f.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Label(f, text= \"fixed 250x120 frame\" , bg= \"#fc6\" ).pack(expand= True ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.scrollbar","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I connect a Scrollbar to a widget?","qh":"How do I connect a Scrollbar to a widget?","a":"Two links: the widget gets <code>yscrollcommand=scrollbar.set</code>, the scrollbar gets <code>command=widget.yview</code>. Works for Text, Listbox, Canvas, Treeview (use <code>x</code> versions for horizontal).","aka":"scrollbar, add scrollbar, scroll, yscrollcommand, xscrollcommand, horizontal scrollbar","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, wrap=<i class=\"s\">&quot;none&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">8</i>)\nys = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>, command=t.yview)\nxs = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, command=t.xview)\nt.config(yscrollcommand=ys.<i class=\"b\">set</i>, xscrollcommand=xs.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\nt.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>)\nys.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ns&quot;</i>)\nxs.grid(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ew&quot;</i>)\nroot.rowconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>); root.columnconfigure(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\nt.insert(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;\\n&quot;</i>.join(<i class=\"s\">&quot;long line &quot;</i> * <i class=\"n\">8</i> + <i class=\"b\">str</i>(i) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">60</i>)))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.scroll-frame","tk.text-scroll","tk.listbox-scroll"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, wrap= \"none\" , width= 30 , height= 8 ) ys = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient= \"vertical\" , command=t.yview) xs = tk.Scrollbar(root, orient= \"horizontal\" , command=t.xview) t.config(yscrollcommand=ys. set , xscrollcommand=xs. set ) t.grid(row= 0 , column= 0 , sticky= \"nsew\" ) ys.grid(row= 0 , column= 1 , sticky= \"ns\" ) xs.grid(row= 1 , column= 0 , sticky= \"ew\" ) root.rowconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ); root.columnconfigure( 0 , weight= 1 ) t.insert( \"1.0\" , \"\\n\" .join( \"long line \" * 8 + str (i) for i in range ( 60 ))) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.scroll-frame","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a scrollable Frame (scroll a whole page of widgets)?","qh":"How do I make a scrollable Frame (scroll a whole page of widgets)?","a":"Frames can't scroll. Put a Frame inside a Canvas with <code>create_window</code>, update the canvas <code>scrollregion</code> when the frame changes size, and attach a Scrollbar to the canvas.","aka":"scrollable frame, scroll many widgets, scroll window, scrolling frame canvas, frame with scrollbar, mousewheel scroll frame","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">ScrollFrame</i>(tk.Frame):\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, master, **kw):\n        <i class=\"b\">super</i>().__init__(master, **kw)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas = tk.Canvas(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n        sb = tk.Scrollbar(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;vertical&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.yview)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner = tk.Frame(<i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.configure(scrollregion=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.bbox(<i class=\"s\">&quot;all&quot;</i>)))\n        win = <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.create_window((<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), window=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner, anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nw&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.itemconfigure(win, width=e.width))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.configure(yscrollcommand=sb.<i class=\"b\">set</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n        sb.pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;right&quot;</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;y&quot;</i>)\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Enter&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.bind_all(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;MouseWheel&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">self</i>._wheel))\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.inner.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Leave&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.unbind_all(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;MouseWheel&gt;&quot;</i>))\n\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">_wheel</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, e):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.canvas.yview_scroll(<i class=\"b\">int</i>(-e.delta / <i class=\"n\">120</i>) <i class=\"k\">or</i> (-<i class=\"n\">1</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> e.delta &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">1</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;units&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x250&quot;</i>)\nsf = ScrollFrame(root)\nsf.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">40</i>):\n    tk.Checkbutton(sf.inner, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Option </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Put your widgets in <code>sf.inner</code>, not <code>sf</code>. On Linux the wheel sends <code>&lt;Button-4&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;Button-5&gt;</code> instead; see <a href=\"#/a/tk.mousewheel\">How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel?</a>.</p>","see":["tk.mousewheel"],"t":"import tkinter as tk class ScrollFrame (tk.Frame): def __init__ ( self , master, **kw): super ().__init__(master, **kw) self .canvas = tk.Canvas( self , highlightthickness= 0 ) sb = tk.Scrollbar( self , orient= \"vertical\" , command= self .canvas.yview) self .inner = tk.Frame( self .canvas) self .inner.bind( \"<Configure>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.configure(scrollregion= self .canvas.bbox( \"all\" ))) win = self .canvas.create_window(( 0 , 0 ), window= self .inner, anchor= \"nw\" ) self .canvas.bind( \"<Configure>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.itemconfigure(win, width=e.width)) self .canvas.configure(yscrollcommand=sb. set ) self .canvas.pack(side= \"left\" , fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) sb.pack(side= \"right\" , fill= \"y\" ) self .inner.bind( \"<Enter>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.bind_all( \"<MouseWheel>\" , self ._wheel)) self .inner.bind( \"<Leave>\" , lambda e: self .canvas.unbind_all( \"<MouseWheel>\" )) def _wheel ( self , e): self .canvas.yview_scroll( int (-e.delta / 120 ) or (- 1 if e.delta > 0 else 1 ), \"units\" ) root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x250\" ) sf = ScrollFrame(root) sf.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) for i in range ( 40 ): tk.Checkbutton(sf.inner, text= f\" Option {i} \" ).pack(anchor= \"w\" ) root.mainloop() Put your widgets in sf.inner , not sf . On Linux the wheel sends <Button-4> / <Button-5> instead; see How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel? ."},{"id":"tk.paned","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make a split view with a draggable divider?","qh":"How do I make a split view with a draggable divider?","a":"Use <code>ttk.PanedWindow</code> (or <code>tk.PanedWindow</code>) and <code>add()</code> each side.","aka":"panedwindow, splitter, resizable panels, split pane, draggable divider","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;400x200&quot;</i>)\npw = ttk.PanedWindow(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>)\npw.pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;both&quot;</i>, expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\npw.add(tk.Listbox(pw), weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i>)\npw.add(tk.Text(pw), weight=<i class=\"n\">3</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"400x200\" ) pw = ttk.PanedWindow(root, orient= \"horizontal\" ) pw.pack(fill= \"both\" , expand= True ) pw.add(tk.Listbox(pw), weight= 1 ) pw.add(tk.Text(pw), weight= 3 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.separator","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I draw a dividing line between widgets?","qh":"How do I draw a dividing line between widgets?","a":"<code>ttk.Separator(root, orient=&quot;horizontal&quot;).pack(fill=&quot;x&quot;)</code>, or a 1-pixel-high Frame with a background colour.","aka":"separator, horizontal line, divider, hr, vertical line between widgets","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">from</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">import</i> ttk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Above&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nttk.Separator(root, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Below&quot;</i>).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Frame(root, height=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#c23b22&quot;</i>).pack(fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;x&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk from tkinter import ttk root = tk.Tk() tk.Label(root, text= \"Above\" ).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 5 ) ttk.Separator(root, orient= \"horizontal\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 5 ) tk.Label(root, text= \"Below\" ).pack(pady= 5 ) tk.Frame(root, height= 2 , bg= \"#c23b22\" ).pack(fill= \"x\" , padx= 10 , pady= 5 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.responsive","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I make my layout adapt when the window is resized?","qh":"How do I make my layout adapt when the window is resized?","a":"Use <code>fill</code>/<code>expand</code> (pack) or <code>weight</code> + <code>sticky</code> (grid) so widgets stretch. For bigger changes (e.g. switch to one column when narrow), bind <code>&lt;Configure&gt;</code> and re-grid.","aka":"responsive layout, resize widgets with window, window resize event, scale widgets, adaptive layout","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;500x200&quot;</i>)\nboxes = [tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Panel </i>{i}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, relief=<i class=\"s\">&quot;solid&quot;</i>, bd=<i class=\"n\">1</i>) <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>)]\nmode = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;cols&quot;</i>: <i class=\"k\">None</i>}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">relayout</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    cols = <i class=\"n\">3</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> root.winfo_width() &gt; <i class=\"n\">400</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> cols == mode[<i class=\"s\">&quot;cols&quot;</i>]:\n        <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    mode[<i class=\"s\">&quot;cols&quot;</i>] = cols\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i, b <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">enumerate</i>(boxes):\n        b.grid(row=i // cols, column=i % cols, sticky=<i class=\"s\">&quot;nsew&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">3</i>):\n        root.columnconfigure(c, weight=<i class=\"n\">1</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> c &lt; cols <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"n\">0</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, relayout)\nrelayout()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.pack-fill","tk.grid-weight"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"500x200\" ) boxes = [tk.Label(root, text= f\" Panel {i} \" , bg= \"#fc6\" , relief= \"solid\" , bd= 1 ) for i in range ( 3 )] mode = { \"cols\" : None } def relayout (event= None ): cols = 3 if root.winfo_width() > 400 else 1 if cols == mode[ \"cols\" ]: return mode[ \"cols\" ] = cols for i, b in enumerate (boxes): b.grid(row=i // cols, column=i % cols, sticky= \"nsew\" , padx= 4 , pady= 4 ) for c in range ( 3 ): root.columnconfigure(c, weight= 1 if c < cols else 0 ) root.bind( \"<Configure>\" , relayout) relayout() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.grid-info","s":"tk-layout","q":"How do I find which row/column a widget is in, or which widget is at a cell?","qh":"How do I find which row/column a widget is in, or which widget is at a cell?","a":"<code>widget.grid_info()</code> returns a dict with <code>&quot;row&quot;</code> and <code>&quot;column&quot;</code>. <code>parent.grid_slaves(row=r, column=c)</code> returns the widgets at that cell.","aka":"grid_info, grid_slaves, widget at row column, get widget position grid","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> r <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>):\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> c <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">2</i>):\n        tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{r}<i class=\"s\">,</i>{c}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>).grid(row=r, column=c)\nw = root.grid_slaves(row=<i class=\"n\">1</i>, column=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)[<i class=\"n\">0</i>]\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(w[<i class=\"s\">&quot;text&quot;</i>], w.grid_info()[<i class=\"s\">&quot;row&quot;</i>], w.grid_info()[<i class=\"s\">&quot;column&quot;</i>])\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() for r in range ( 2 ): for c in range ( 2 ): tk.Label(root, text= f\" {r} , {c} \" , width= 6 ).grid(row=r, column=c) w = root.grid_slaves(row= 1 , column= 0 )[ 0 ] print (w[ \"text\" ], w.grid_info()[ \"row\" ], w.grid_info()[ \"column\" ]) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.bind","s":"tk-events","q":"How does bind() work?","qh":"How does bind() work?","a":"<code>widget.bind(&quot;&lt;Event&gt;&quot;, function)</code>. The function receives one <code>event</code> argument with details (<code>event.x</code>, <code>event.keysym</code>, <code>event.widget</code>...).","aka":"bind event, event binding, event handler, bind function, widget.bind, event callback","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Click or type&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">clicked</i>(event):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Click at </i>{event.x}<i class=\"s\">, </i>{event.y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">typed</i>(event):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Key: </i>{event.keysym}<i class=\"s\">  char: </i>{event.char!r}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, clicked)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</i>, typed)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>A function used for both <code>command=</code> (no argument) and <code>bind</code> (one argument) should be written <code>def f(event=None):</code>.</p>","see":["tk.bind-keys","tk.mouse","tk.event-names"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Click or type\" , width= 30 , height= 5 , bg= \"#eee\" ) lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def clicked (event): lab.config(text= f\" Click at {event.x} , {event.y} \" ) def typed (event): lab.config(text= f\" Key: {event.keysym} char: {event.char!r} \" ) lab.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , clicked) root.bind( \"<Key>\" , typed) root.mainloop() A function used for both command= (no argument) and bind (one argument) should be written def f(event=None): ."},{"id":"tk.err.bind-missing-arg","s":"tk-events","q":"TypeError: my_function() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given (when using bind)","qh":"TypeError: my_function() takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given (when using bind)","a":"<code>bind</code> always passes an <code>event</code> argument. Add a parameter: <code>def go(event=None):</code>. (In a class method, <code>self</code> is the first one, so you need <code>def go(self, event=None):</code>.)","aka":"bind takes 0 positional arguments, bind missing event argument, function takes no arguments but 1 given tkinter","err":"takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given|takes 1 positional argument but 2 were given","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">go</i>():\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;go&quot;</i>)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, go)     <i class=\"c\"># TypeError when Enter is pressed</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">go</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;go&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, go)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Go&quot;</i>, command=go).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">40</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.bind","py.err.positional-args"],"t":"def go (): print ( \"go\" ) root.bind( \"<Return>\" , go) # TypeError when Enter is pressed import tkinter as tk def go (event= None ): print ( \"go\" ) root = tk.Tk() root.bind( \"<Return>\" , go) tk.Button(root, text= \"Go\" , command=go).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 40 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.bind-keys","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I detect key presses (arrow keys, letters, Enter, Escape)?","qh":"How do I detect key presses (arrow keys, letters, Enter, Escape)?","a":"Bind on the window: <code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Left&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, <code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, <code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Escape&gt;&quot;, f)</code>, <code>root.bind(&quot;a&quot;, f)</code>, or <code>&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</code> for every key and check <code>event.keysym</code>.","aka":"keyboard input, key press event, arrow keys, keypress, detect key, keyboard controls, wasd, KeyPress","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">240</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">160</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\ndot = c.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">110</i>, <i class=\"n\">70</i>, <i class=\"n\">130</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>)\nmoves = {<i class=\"s\">&quot;Left&quot;</i>: (-<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Right&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Up&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">10</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;Down&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>),\n         <i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>: (-<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;d&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">10</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">10</i>), <i class=\"s\">&quot;s&quot;</i>: (<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>)}\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">key</i>(event):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> event.keysym <i class=\"k\">in</i> moves:\n        c.move(dot, *moves[event.keysym])\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</i>, key)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Key events go to the widget that has <b>focus</b>. Binding on <code>root</code> catches keys anywhere in that window. If nothing happens, click the window first.</p>","see":["tk.shortcuts","tk.key-held","tk.event-names"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 240 , height= 160 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() dot = c.create_oval( 110 , 70 , 130 , 90 , fill= \"red\" ) moves = { \"Left\" : (- 10 , 0 ), \"Right\" : ( 10 , 0 ), \"Up\" : ( 0 , - 10 ), \"Down\" : ( 0 , 10 ), \"a\" : (- 10 , 0 ), \"d\" : ( 10 , 0 ), \"w\" : ( 0 , - 10 ), \"s\" : ( 0 , 10 )} def key (event): if event.keysym in moves: c.move(dot, *moves[event.keysym]) root.bind( \"<Key>\" , key) root.mainloop() Key events go to the widget that has focus . Binding on root catches keys anywhere in that window. If nothing happens, click the window first."},{"id":"tk.key-held","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I make smooth movement while a key is held down (for games)?","qh":"How do I make smooth movement while a key is held down (for games)?","a":"Don't move on each key event (it stutters and has a repeat delay). Track which keys are down with <code>&lt;KeyPress&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;KeyRelease&gt;</code> in a set, and move in an <a href=\"#/a/tk.after\">after()</a> game loop.","aka":"key held down, smooth movement, keyrelease, multiple keys at once, game controls, key repeat delay","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nship = c.create_rectangle(<i class=\"n\">140</i>, <i class=\"n\">90</i>, <i class=\"n\">160</i>, <i class=\"n\">110</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;orange&quot;</i>)\nheld = <i class=\"b\">set</i>()\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;KeyPress&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: held.add(e.keysym))\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;KeyRelease&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: held.discard(e.keysym))\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>():\n    dx = ((<i class=\"s\">&quot;Right&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held) - (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Left&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held)) * <i class=\"n\">4</i>\n    dy = ((<i class=\"s\">&quot;Down&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held) - (<i class=\"s\">&quot;Up&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">in</i> held)) * <i class=\"n\">4</i>\n    c.move(ship, dx, dy)\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">16</i>, tick)          <i class=\"c\"># about 60 times a second</i>\n\ntick()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.game-loop"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 200 , bg= \"black\" ) c.pack() ship = c.create_rectangle( 140 , 90 , 160 , 110 , fill= \"orange\" ) held = set () root.bind( \"<KeyPress>\" , lambda e: held.add(e.keysym)) root.bind( \"<KeyRelease>\" , lambda e: held.discard(e.keysym)) def tick (): dx = (( \"Right\" in held) - ( \"Left\" in held)) * 4 dy = (( \"Down\" in held) - ( \"Up\" in held)) * 4 c.move(ship, dx, dy) root.after( 16 , tick) # about 60 times a second tick() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.shortcuts","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I add keyboard shortcuts like Ctrl+S?","qh":"How do I add keyboard shortcuts like Ctrl+S?","a":"<code>root.bind(&quot;&lt;Control-s&gt;&quot;, save)</code>. Shift is a different key: <code>&quot;&lt;Control-S&gt;&quot;</code> (capital) is Ctrl+Shift+S. On macOS use <code>&quot;&lt;Command-s&gt;&quot;</code>.","aka":"keyboard shortcut, hotkey, ctrl s, control key binding, command key mac, accelerator, ctrl+c ctrl+v","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstatus = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Press Ctrl+S or Ctrl+O&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nstatus.pack()\nmod = <i class=\"s\">&quot;Command&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;darwin&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Control&quot;</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">save</i>(event=<i class=\"k\">None</i>):\n    status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Saved&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;break&quot;</i>          <i class=\"c\"># stop the key doing anything else</i>\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">&lt;</i>{mod}<i class=\"s\">-s&gt;</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, save)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">&lt;</i>{mod}<i class=\"s\">-o&gt;</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: status.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Open&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.menu-accelerator"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys root = tk.Tk() status = tk.Label(root, text= \"Press Ctrl+S or Ctrl+O\" , width= 30 , pady= 20 ) status.pack() mod = \"Command\" if sys.platform == \"darwin\" else \"Control\" def save (event= None ): status.config(text= \"Saved\" ) return \"break\" # stop the key doing anything else root.bind( f\" < {mod} -s> \" , save) root.bind( f\" < {mod} -o> \" , lambda e: status.config(text= \"Open\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.mouse","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I detect mouse clicks, right-clicks and mouse position?","qh":"How do I detect mouse clicks, right-clicks and mouse position?","a":"<code>&lt;Button-1&gt;</code> left, <code>&lt;Button-2&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;Button-3&gt;</code> middle/right (on macOS right-click is <code>&lt;Button-2&gt;</code>), <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> when moving. <code>event.x, event.y</code> are relative to the widget; <code>event.x_root, event.y_root</code> to the screen.","aka":"mouse click event, right click, middle click, mouse position, mouse coordinates, Button-1, Button-3, cursor position","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> sys\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=<i class=\"n\">300</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">200</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\npos = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>)\npos.pack()\nright = <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-2&gt;&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> sys.platform == <i class=\"s\">&quot;darwin&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-3&gt;&quot;</i>\n\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: pos.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">x=</i>{e.x}<i class=\"s\"> y=</i>{e.y}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>))\nc.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: c.create_oval(e.x-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.x+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;blue&quot;</i>))\nc.bind(right, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: c.create_oval(e.x-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y-<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.x+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, e.y+<i class=\"n\">4</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>To get the mouse position at any moment: <code>root.winfo_pointerxy()</code> (screen coordinates).</p>","see":["tk.drag","tk.right-click-menu"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import sys root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width= 300 , height= 200 , bg= \"white\" ) c.pack() pos = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" ) pos.pack() right = \"<Button-2>\" if sys.platform == \"darwin\" else \"<Button-3>\" c.bind( \"<Motion>\" , lambda e: pos.config(text= f\" x= {e.x} y= {e.y} \" )) c.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , lambda e: c.create_oval(e.x- 4 , e.y- 4 , e.x+ 4 , e.y+ 4 , fill= \"blue\" )) c.bind(right, lambda e: c.create_oval(e.x- 4 , e.y- 4 , e.x+ 4 , e.y+ 4 , fill= \"red\" )) root.mainloop() To get the mouse position at any moment: root.winfo_pointerxy() (screen coordinates)."},{"id":"tk.double-click","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I detect a double-click?","qh":"How do I detect a double-click?","a":"Bind <code>&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</code>. Note the single-click handler also runs on the first click.","aka":"double click event, Double-Button-1, double tap","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Double-click me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Double!&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Double-click me\" , bg= \"#eee\" , padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) lab.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) lab.bind( \"<Double-Button-1>\" , lambda e: lab.config(text= \"Double!\" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.drag","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I drag something with the mouse?","qh":"How do I drag something with the mouse?","a":"On <code>&lt;Button-1&gt;</code> remember the start point; on <code>&lt;B1-Motion&gt;</code> move by the difference and update the start point.","aka":"drag and drop, drag widget, drag item canvas, move with mouse, B1-Motion, click and drag","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.geometry(<i class=\"s\">&quot;300x200&quot;</i>)\ncard = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;drag me&quot;</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#fc6&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">6</i>)\ncard.place(x=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, y=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>(e):\n    card._x, card._y = e.x, e.y\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">move</i>(e):\n    card.place(x=card.winfo_x() + e.x - card._x, y=card.winfo_y() + e.y - card._y)\n\ncard.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, start)\ncard.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;B1-Motion&gt;&quot;</i>, move)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Dragging <b>files from your desktop</b> into tkinter isn't built in; the <code>tkinterdnd2</code> package adds it.</p>","see":["tk.canvas-drag","tk.drag-window"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() root.geometry( \"300x200\" ) card = tk.Label(root, text= \"drag me\" , bg= \"#fc6\" , padx= 10 , pady= 6 ) card.place(x= 20 , y= 20 ) def start (e): card._x, card._y = e.x, e.y def move (e): card.place(x=card.winfo_x() + e.x - card._x, y=card.winfo_y() + e.y - card._y) card.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , start) card.bind( \"<B1-Motion>\" , move) root.mainloop() Dragging files from your desktop into tkinter isn't built in; the tkinterdnd2 package adds it."},{"id":"tk.mousewheel","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel?","qh":"How do I use the mouse wheel / scroll wheel?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;MouseWheel&gt;</code> and look at <code>event.delta</code> (positive = up). Older Tk on Linux sends <code>&lt;Button-4&gt;</code> (up) and <code>&lt;Button-5&gt;</code> (down) instead, so bind those too.","aka":"mouse wheel event, scroll wheel, MouseWheel, Button-4 Button-5, scroll linux, zoom with wheel","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nn = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">6</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">wheel</i>(event):\n    up = event.num == <i class=\"n\">4</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> <i class=\"b\">getattr</i>(event, <i class=\"s\">&quot;delta&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>) &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i>\n    n.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(n.get() + (<i class=\"n\">1</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> up <i class=\"k\">else</i> -<i class=\"n\">1</i>))\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;MouseWheel&gt;&quot;</i>, wheel)     <i class=\"c\"># Windows, macOS, Tk 9 on Linux</i>\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-4&gt;&quot;</i>, wheel)       <i class=\"c\"># Linux, Tk 8.6</i>\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-5&gt;&quot;</i>, wheel)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Windows gives <code>delta</code> in steps of 120; macOS gives small numbers like 1 or 3.</p>","see":["tk.scroll-frame"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() n = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=( \"Arial\" , 30 ), width= 6 ).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) def wheel (event): up = event.num == 4 or getattr (event, \"delta\" , 0 ) > 0 n. set (n.get() + ( 1 if up else - 1 )) root.bind( \"<MouseWheel>\" , wheel) # Windows, macOS, Tk 9 on Linux root.bind( \"<Button-4>\" , wheel) # Linux, Tk 8.6 root.bind( \"<Button-5>\" , wheel) root.mainloop() Windows gives delta in steps of 120; macOS gives small numbers like 1 or 3."},{"id":"tk.event-names","s":"tk-events","q":"What event names can I bind?","qh":"What event names can I bind?","a":"Common ones below. Format: <code>&lt;Modifier-Type-Detail&gt;</code>, e.g. <code>&lt;Control-Shift-Key-a&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;</code>.","aka":"list of events, event types, bind strings, event sequence, keysym names, event syntax","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code out\" data-tag=\"output\"><pre>&lt;Button-1&gt; &lt;Button-3&gt;        mouse button pressed (1 left, 2 middle, 3 right; mac right = 2)\n&lt;ButtonRelease-1&gt;            mouse button released\n&lt;Double-Button-1&gt;            double-click\n&lt;B1-Motion&gt;                  mouse moved with left button held (dragging)\n&lt;Motion&gt;                     mouse moved\n&lt;Enter&gt; &lt;Leave&gt;              mouse moved into / out of the widget\n&lt;MouseWheel&gt;                 scroll wheel (Linux Tk 8.6: &lt;Button-4&gt;/&lt;Button-5&gt;)\n&lt;Key&gt; &lt;KeyPress&gt;             any key;  &lt;KeyRelease&gt; any key released\n&lt;Return&gt; &lt;Escape&gt; &lt;Tab&gt; &lt;BackSpace&gt; &lt;Delete&gt; &lt;space&gt;\n&lt;Up&gt; &lt;Down&gt; &lt;Left&gt; &lt;Right&gt; &lt;Home&gt; &lt;End&gt; &lt;Prior&gt;(PageUp) &lt;Next&gt;(PageDown)\n&lt;F1&gt; ... &lt;F12&gt;\na   A   1                    a single character (A means Shift+a)\n&lt;Control-s&gt; &lt;Alt-x&gt; &lt;Shift-Up&gt; &lt;Command-s&gt;(mac)\n&lt;FocusIn&gt; &lt;FocusOut&gt;         widget gained / lost keyboard focus\n&lt;Configure&gt;                  widget resized or moved (event.width, event.height)\n&lt;Destroy&gt;                    widget destroyed\n&lt;Map&gt; &lt;Unmap&gt;                shown / hidden\n&lt;&lt;ComboboxSelected&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;ListboxSelect&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;TreeviewSelect&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;NotebookTabChanged&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;Modified&gt;&gt;</pre></figure><p>To find the name of any key, bind <code>&lt;Key&gt;</code> and print <code>event.keysym</code>.</p>","see":["tk.bind"],"t":"<Button-1> <Button-3> mouse button pressed (1 left, 2 middle, 3 right; mac right = 2) <ButtonRelease-1> mouse button released <Double-Button-1> double-click <B1-Motion> mouse moved with left button held (dragging) <Motion> mouse moved <Enter> <Leave> mouse moved into / out of the widget <MouseWheel> scroll wheel (Linux Tk 8.6: <Button-4>/<Button-5>) <Key> <KeyPress> any key; <KeyRelease> any key released <Return> <Escape> <Tab> <BackSpace> <Delete> <space> <Up> <Down> <Left> <Right> <Home> <End> <Prior>(PageUp) <Next>(PageDown) <F1> ... <F12> a A 1 a single character (A means Shift+a) <Control-s> <Alt-x> <Shift-Up> <Command-s>(mac) <FocusIn> <FocusOut> widget gained / lost keyboard focus <Configure> widget resized or moved (event.width, event.height) <Destroy> widget destroyed <Map> <Unmap> shown / hidden <<ComboboxSelected>> <<ListboxSelect>> <<TreeviewSelect>> <<NotebookTabChanged>> <<Modified>> To find the name of any key, bind <Key> and print event.keysym ."},{"id":"tk.event-attrs","s":"tk-events","q":"What information does the event object have?","qh":"What information does the event object have?","a":"The useful fields: <code>widget</code>, <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>, <code>x_root</code>, <code>y_root</code>, <code>keysym</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>keycode</code>, <code>num</code> (mouse button), <code>delta</code> (wheel), <code>width</code>, <code>height</code> (Configure), <code>state</code> (modifier keys).","aka":"event object, event attributes, event.widget, event.keysym, event.char, event.x","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;press a key or click&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nlab.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">show</i>(e):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">widget=</i>{e.widget.winfo_class()}<i class=\"s\"> keysym=</i>{e.keysym}<i class=\"s\"> char=</i>{e.char!r}<i class=\"s\">\\nx=</i>{e.x}<i class=\"s\"> y=</i>{e.y}<i class=\"s\"> num=</i>{e.num}<i class=\"s\"> state=</i>{e.state}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Key&gt;&quot;</i>, show)\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button&gt;&quot;</i>, show)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>event.widget</code> is the widget it happened on: handy when one function handles many widgets.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"press a key or click\" , width= 40 , height= 4 ) lab.pack() def show (e): lab.config(text= f\" widget= {e.widget.winfo_class()} keysym= {e.keysym} char= {e.char!r} \\nx= {e.x} y= {e.y} num= {e.num} state= {e.state} \" ) root.bind( \"<Key>\" , show) root.bind( \"<Button>\" , show) root.mainloop() event.widget is the widget it happened on: handy when one function handles many widgets."},{"id":"tk.return-break","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I stop a key doing its normal thing (e.g. stop Enter adding a new line in a Text)?","qh":"How do I stop a key doing its normal thing (e.g. stop Enter adding a new line in a Text)?","a":"Return the string <code>&quot;break&quot;</code> from your handler. Tk then skips the widget's built-in behaviour for that event.","aka":"return break, prevent default, block key, stop newline text widget, override key behaviour","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nt = tk.Text(root, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nt.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">send</i>(event):\n    <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Sent:&quot;</i>, t.get(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end-1c&quot;</i>))\n    t.delete(<i class=\"s\">&quot;1.0&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;end&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">return</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;break&quot;</i>           <i class=\"c\"># no newline gets inserted</i>\n\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Return&gt;&quot;</i>, send)\nt.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Shift-Return&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"k\">None</i>)   <i class=\"c\"># Shift+Enter still makes a new line</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() t = tk.Text(root, width= 30 , height= 5 ) t.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def send (event): print ( \"Sent:\" , t.get( \"1.0\" , \"end-1c\" )) t.delete( \"1.0\" , \"end\" ) return \"break\" # no newline gets inserted t.bind( \"<Return>\" , send) t.bind( \"<Shift-Return>\" , lambda e: None ) # Shift+Enter still makes a new line root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.unbind","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I remove a binding (unbind)?","qh":"How do I remove a binding (unbind)?","a":"<code>widget.unbind(&quot;&lt;Event&gt;&quot;)</code>. To remove only one of several handlers, keep the id that <code>bind(..., add=&quot;+&quot;)</code> returns and pass it as the second argument.","aka":"unbind, remove event, stop listening to key, disable bind","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Click me once&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#eee&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">once</i>(e):\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clicked (now unbound)&quot;</i>)\n    lab.unbind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>)\n\nlab.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Button-1&gt;&quot;</i>, once)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>bind</code> normally <b>replaces</b> the previous handler for that event; pass <code>add=&quot;+&quot;</code> to keep both.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"Click me once\" , padx= 30 , pady= 30 , bg= \"#eee\" ) lab.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 20 ) def once (e): lab.config(text= \"Clicked (now unbound)\" ) lab.unbind( \"<Button-1>\" ) lab.bind( \"<Button-1>\" , once) root.mainloop() bind normally replaces the previous handler for that event; pass add=\"+\" to keep both."},{"id":"tk.bind-all","s":"tk-events","q":"What's the difference between bind, bind_all and bind_class?","qh":"What's the difference between bind, bind_all and bind_class?","a":"<code>w.bind</code> is for one widget. <code>bind_all</code> fires in every widget of the app. <code>bind_class(&quot;Entry&quot;, ...)</code> affects every widget of that type.","aka":"bind_all, bind_class, global key binding, bind to every widget","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ntk.Entry(root).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Entry(root).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.bind_all(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;F1&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Help (from anywhere)&quot;</i>))\nroot.bind_class(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Entry&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusIn&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: e.widget.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffffe0&quot;</i>), add=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>)\nroot.bind_class(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Entry&quot;</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusOut&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: e.widget.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>), add=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+&quot;</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() tk.Entry(root).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Entry(root).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) root.bind_all( \"<F1>\" , lambda e: print ( \"Help (from anywhere)\" )) root.bind_class( \"Entry\" , \"<FocusIn>\" , lambda e: e.widget.config(bg= \"#ffffe0\" ), add= \"+\" ) root.bind_class( \"Entry\" , \"<FocusOut>\" , lambda e: e.widget.config(bg= \"white\" ), add= \"+\" ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.focus","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I tell which widget has focus, or react when it gains/loses focus?","qh":"How do I tell which widget has focus, or react when it gains/loses focus?","a":"<code>root.focus_get()</code> returns the focused widget. Bind <code>&lt;FocusIn&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;FocusOut&gt;</code> to react.","aka":"focus_get, focus in, focus out, FocusIn, FocusOut, focused widget, on blur","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\na = tk.Entry(root); a.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nb = tk.Entry(root); b.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\n<i class=\"k\">for</i> e <i class=\"k\">in</i> (a, b):\n    e.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;FocusIn&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> ev: ev.widget.config(highlightthickness=<i class=\"n\">2</i>, highlightcolor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;orange&quot;</i>))\na.focus()\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;focused:&quot;</i>, root.focus_get() <i class=\"k\">is</i> a))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() a = tk.Entry(root); a.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) b = tk.Entry(root); b.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) for e in (a, b): e.bind( \"<FocusIn>\" , lambda ev: ev.widget.config(highlightthickness= 2 , highlightcolor= \"orange\" )) a.focus() root.after( 100 , lambda : print ( \"focused:\" , root.focus_get() is a)) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.virtual-event","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I create my own event (virtual event) and trigger it?","qh":"How do I create my own event (virtual event) and trigger it?","a":"Bind a name in double angle brackets, <code>&lt;&lt;MyEvent&gt;&gt;</code>, and fire it with <code>widget.event_generate(&quot;&lt;&lt;MyEvent&gt;&gt;&quot;)</code>. <code>event_generate</code> also simulates real events.","aka":"event_generate, custom event, virtual event, trigger event programmatically, simulate key press","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;waiting&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack()\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Scored&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i> e: lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Somebody scored!&quot;</i>))\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Score&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: root.event_generate(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;&lt;Scored&gt;&gt;&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"waiting\" , padx= 30 , pady= 30 ) lab.pack() root.bind( \"<<Scored>>\" , lambda e: lab.config(text= \"Somebody scored!\" )) tk.Button(root, text= \"Score\" , command= lambda : root.event_generate( \"<<Scored>>\" )).pack(pady= 10 ) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.configure-event","s":"tk-events","q":"How do I run code when the window is resized?","qh":"How do I run code when the window is resized?","a":"Bind <code>&lt;Configure&gt;</code> on the window. It also fires when the window moves, and for child widgets, so check <code>event.widget</code>.","aka":"window resize event, Configure event, on resize, detect window size change","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;resize me&quot;</i>)\nlab.pack(expand=<i class=\"k\">True</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">resized</i>(e):\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> e.widget <i class=\"k\">is</i> root:\n        lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{e.width}<i class=\"s\"> x </i>{e.height}<i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n\nroot.bind(<i class=\"s\">&quot;&lt;Configure&gt;&quot;</i>, resized)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"resize me\" ) lab.pack(expand= True ) def resized (e): if e.widget is root: lab.config(text= f\" {e.width} x {e.height} \" ) root.bind( \"<Configure>\" , resized) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.stringvar","s":"tk-vars","q":"What is StringVar and when should I use it?","qh":"What is StringVar and when should I use it?","a":"A <code>StringVar</code> holds a value that widgets can watch. Link it with <code>textvariable=</code> (Label, Entry) or <code>variable=</code> (Checkbutton, Radiobutton, Scale); then <code>var.set(...)</code> updates every linked widget, and <code>var.get()</code> reads it.","aka":"StringVar, textvariable, tkinter variable, IntVar, DoubleVar, BooleanVar, control variable, tk variables","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;world&quot;</i>)\ntk.Entry(root, textvariable=name).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=name, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">16</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># changes as you type</i>\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Reset&quot;</i>, command=<i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: name.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;world&quot;</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">5</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>You don't <b>need</b> them; <code>widget.config(text=...)</code> and <code>entry.get()</code> do the same. They're handy when several widgets show one value.</p>","see":["tk.trace","tk.var-types"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.StringVar(value= \"world\" ) tk.Entry(root, textvariable=name).pack(padx= 20 , pady= 5 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=name, font=( \"Arial\" , 16 )).pack(pady= 5 ) # changes as you type tk.Button(root, text= \"Reset\" , command= lambda : name. set ( \"world\" )).pack(pady= 5 ) root.mainloop() You don't need them; widget.config(text=...) and entry.get() do the same. They're handy when several widgets show one value."},{"id":"tk.var-types","s":"tk-vars","q":"What's the difference between StringVar, IntVar, DoubleVar and BooleanVar?","qh":"What's the difference between StringVar, IntVar, DoubleVar and BooleanVar?","a":"They differ only in what <code>get()</code> returns: <code>str</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>bool</code>. If the linked Entry holds text that isn't a number, <code>IntVar.get()</code> raises <code>TclError</code>.","aka":"IntVar, DoubleVar, BooleanVar, var get type, intvar error, expected floating-point number","err":"TclError: expected floating-point number but got|expected integer but got","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nage = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">12</i>)\ne = tk.Entry(root, textvariable=age)\ne.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">check</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Next year:&quot;</i>, age.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        <i class=\"b\">print</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;That&#x27;s not a whole number&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Check&quot;</i>, command=check).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>For user-typed numbers, a StringVar + your own <code>int()</code> in <code>try</code> is often simpler.</p>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() age = tk.IntVar(value= 12 ) e = tk.Entry(root, textvariable=age) e.pack(padx= 20 , pady= 10 ) def check (): try : print ( \"Next year:\" , age.get() + 1 ) except tk.TclError: print ( \"That's not a whole number\" ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Check\" , command=check).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop() For user-typed numbers, a StringVar + your own int() in try is often simpler."},{"id":"tk.trace","s":"tk-vars","q":"How do I run a function whenever a variable changes (trace)?","qh":"How do I run a function whenever a variable changes (trace)?","a":"<code>var.trace_add(&quot;write&quot;, callback)</code>. The callback receives three arguments you can ignore with <code>*args</code>. (<code>trace(&quot;w&quot;, ...)</code> is the old, deprecated form.)","aka":"trace_add, trace variable, on change, watch variable, trace write, trace_variable deprecated","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nf = tk.DoubleVar(value=<i class=\"n\">32</i>)\nc = tk.StringVar()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">convert</i>(*args):\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        c.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i>{(f.get() - <i class=\"n\">32</i>) * <i class=\"n\">5</i> / <i class=\"n\">9</i>:<i class=\"s\">.1f</i>}<i class=\"s\"> °C</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> tk.TclError:\n        c.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(<i class=\"s\">&quot;?&quot;</i>)\n\nf.trace_add(<i class=\"s\">&quot;write&quot;</i>, convert)\ntk.Scale(root, from_=-<i class=\"n\">40</i>, to=<i class=\"n\">212</i>, orient=<i class=\"s\">&quot;horizontal&quot;</i>, variable=f, length=<i class=\"n\">240</i>, label=<i class=\"s\">&quot;°F&quot;</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=c, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">18</i>)).pack(pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nconvert()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.entry-changes"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() f = tk.DoubleVar(value= 32 ) c = tk.StringVar() def convert (*args): try : c. set ( f\" {(f.get() - 32 ) * 5 / 9 : .1f } °C \" ) except tk.TclError: c. set ( \"?\" ) f.trace_add( \"write\" , convert) tk.Scale(root, from_=- 40 , to= 212 , orient= \"horizontal\" , variable=f, length= 240 , label= \"°F\" ).pack(padx= 10 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=c, font=( \"Arial\" , 18 )).pack(pady= 10 ) convert() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.var-gc","s":"tk-vars","q":"Why does my Checkbutton/Radiobutton/Label not show the variable's value (it looks empty or all selected)?","qh":"Why does my Checkbutton/Radiobutton/Label not show the variable's value (it looks empty or all selected)?","a":"The <code>StringVar</code> was created inside a function as a local variable and got deleted when the function ended. Store it somewhere that lives on: a global, <code>self.var</code> in a class, or a list/dict.","aka":"variable garbage collected, variable in function, radiobutton all selected, checkbutton not working in function, StringVar local variable","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">build</i>():\n    choice = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)      <i class=\"c\"># deleted when build() ends</i>\n    tk.Radiobutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;A&quot;</i>, variable=choice, value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>).pack()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\n<i class=\"k\">class</i> <i class=\"f\">App</i>:\n    <i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">__init__</i>(<i class=\"k\">self</i>, root):\n        <i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;a&quot;</i>)     <i class=\"c\"># kept alive by self</i>\n        <i class=\"k\">for</i> v <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;abc&quot;</i>:\n            tk.Radiobutton(root, text=v.upper(), variable=<i class=\"k\">self</i>.choice, value=v).pack(anchor=<i class=\"s\">&quot;w&quot;</i>)\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\napp = App(root)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.err.image-gone"],"t":"def build (): choice = tk.StringVar(value= \"a\" ) # deleted when build() ends tk.Radiobutton(root, text= \"A\" , variable=choice, value= \"a\" ).pack() import tkinter as tk class App : def __init__ ( self , root): self .choice = tk.StringVar(value= \"a\" ) # kept alive by self for v in \"abc\" : tk.Radiobutton(root, text=v.upper(), variable= self .choice, value=v).pack(anchor= \"w\" ) root = tk.Tk() app = App(root) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.var-before-root","s":"tk-vars","q":"RuntimeError: Too early to create variable: no default root window","qh":"RuntimeError: Too early to create variable: no default root window","a":"You created a <code>StringVar</code>, <code>PhotoImage</code> or <code>Font</code> before <code>root = tk.Tk()</code>. Create the root window first.","aka":"too early to create variable, no default root window, StringVar before Tk, too early to create image, too early to use font","err":"Too early to create variable|no default root window|Too early to create image|Too early to use font","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\nname = tk.StringVar()     <i class=\"c\"># RuntimeError: no window yet</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()</pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nname = tk.StringVar(value=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ok&quot;</i>)\n<i class=\"b\">print</i>(name.get())\nroot.destroy()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk name = tk.StringVar() # RuntimeError: no window yet root = tk.Tk() import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() name = tk.StringVar(value= \"ok\" ) print (name.get()) root.destroy()"},{"id":"tk.global-vs-class","s":"tk-vars","q":"How do I share or change values between my functions (global, nonlocal, or a class)?","qh":"How do I share or change values between my functions (global, nonlocal, or a class)?","a":"For small scripts, declare <code>global score</code> in the function that changes it. For anything bigger, keep state on a class (<code>self.score</code>) or a dict; it scales better.","aka":"global variable tkinter, change variable in function, score variable, counter button, UnboundLocalError tkinter, share data between functions","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nscore = <i class=\"n\">0</i>\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;0&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">24</i>))\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">add</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> score            <i class=\"c\"># without this: UnboundLocalError</i>\n    score += <i class=\"n\">1</i>\n    lab.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(score))\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;+1&quot;</i>, command=add).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.app-class","py.global"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() score = 0 lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"0\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 24 )) lab.pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) def add (): global score # without this: UnboundLocalError score += 1 lab.config(text= str (score)) tk.Button(root, text= \"+1\" , command=add).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.booleanvar","s":"tk-vars","q":"How do I use BooleanVar with a Checkbutton?","qh":"How do I use BooleanVar with a Checkbutton?","a":"<code>v = tk.BooleanVar()</code>, <code>Checkbutton(..., variable=v)</code>. <code>v.get()</code> gives <code>True</code>/<code>False</code>; <code>v.set(True)</code> ticks it from code.","aka":"booleanvar, checkbox true false, toggle variable","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ndark = tk.BooleanVar()\narea = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Preview&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">24</i>, height=<i class=\"n\">4</i>, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>)\narea.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">apply</i>():\n    area.config(bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#222&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> dark.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;white&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> dark.get() <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>)\n\ntk.Checkbutton(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Dark mode&quot;</i>, variable=dark, command=apply).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.checkbutton"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() dark = tk.BooleanVar() area = tk.Label(root, text= \"Preview\" , width= 24 , height= 4 , bg= \"white\" ) area.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) def apply (): area.config(bg= \"#222\" if dark.get() else \"white\" , fg= \"white\" if dark.get() else \"black\" ) tk.Checkbutton(root, text= \"Dark mode\" , variable=dark, command=apply).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.after","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I run something after a delay, or repeat it every second?","qh":"How do I run something after a delay, or repeat it every second?","a":"<code>root.after(ms, function)</code> runs the function once after <code>ms</code> milliseconds. To repeat, the function schedules itself again at its end.","aka":"after, timer, delay, schedule, repeat every second, set interval, set timeout, loop in tkinter, run function every x seconds","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\ncount = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=count, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>():\n    count.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(count.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, tick)        <i class=\"c\"># call me again in 1 second</i>\n\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, tick)            <i class=\"c\"># first call</i>\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Pass arguments after the function: <code>root.after(500, move, 10, 0)</code> calls <code>move(10, 0)</code>.</p>","see":["tk.after-not-sleep","tk.after-cancel","tk.clock"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() count = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) tk.Label(root, textvariable=count, font=( \"Arial\" , 40 ), width= 4 ).pack(padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) def tick (): count. set (count.get() + 1 ) root.after( 1000 , tick) # call me again in 1 second root.after( 1000 , tick) # first call root.mainloop() Pass arguments after the function: root.after(500, move, 10, 0) calls move(10, 0) ."},{"id":"tk.after-not-sleep","s":"tk-time","q":"Why does time.sleep() freeze my window (\"Not Responding\")?","qh":"Why does time.sleep() freeze my window (\"Not Responding\")?","a":"<code>sleep</code> (and long loops) block <code>mainloop</code>, so the window can't redraw or respond. Use <a href=\"#/a/tk.after\">after()</a> for delays and repeating steps, or a <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">thread</a> for long work.","aka":"time.sleep tkinter, window frozen, not responding, gui freezes, while loop freezes tkinter, sleep in tkinter, label doesn't update until end","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code bad\" data-tag=\"wrong\"><pre><i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">countdown</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">5</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, -<i class=\"n\">1</i>):\n        label.config(text=i)\n        time.sleep(<i class=\"n\">1</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># window freezes, shows only the end</i></pre></figure><figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlabel = tk.Label(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">40</i>), width=<i class=\"n\">4</i>)\nlabel.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">countdown</i>(i=<i class=\"n\">5</i>):\n    label.config(text=<i class=\"b\">str</i>(i) <i class=\"k\">if</i> i <i class=\"k\">else</i> <i class=\"s\">&quot;Go!&quot;</i>)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> i &gt; <i class=\"n\">0</i>:\n        root.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, countdown, i - <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n\ncountdown()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.after","tk.thread","tk.update"],"t":"def countdown (): for i in range ( 5 , 0 , - 1 ): label.config(text=i) time.sleep( 1 ) # window freezes, shows only the end import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() label = tk.Label(root, font=( \"Arial\" , 40 ), width= 4 ) label.pack(padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) def countdown (i= 5 ): label.config(text= str (i) if i else \"Go!\" ) if i > 0 : root.after( 1000 , countdown, i - 1 ) countdown() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.after-cancel","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I stop an after() loop (cancel a timer)?","qh":"How do I stop an after() loop (cancel a timer)?","a":"<code>after()</code> returns an id. Keep it and call <code>root.after_cancel(id)</code>. Or use a flag the loop checks.","aka":"after_cancel, stop timer, stop loop, cancel after, pause timer, stop animation","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nn = tk.IntVar(value=<i class=\"n\">0</i>)\njob = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\ntk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">30</i>)).pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">tick</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> job\n    n.<i class=\"b\">set</i>(n.get() + <i class=\"n\">1</i>)\n    job = root.after(<i class=\"n\">200</i>, tick)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> job <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n        tick()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">stop</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> job\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> job <i class=\"k\">is</i> <i class=\"k\">not</i> <i class=\"k\">None</i>:\n        root.after_cancel(job)\n        job = <i class=\"k\">None</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Start&quot;</i>, command=start).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Stop&quot;</i>, command=stop).pack(side=<i class=\"s\">&quot;left&quot;</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Pressing Start twice without the <code>if job is None</code> check starts two loops that run at double speed.</p>","see":["tk.recipe-stopwatch"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() n = tk.IntVar(value= 0 ) job = None tk.Label(root, textvariable=n, font=( \"Arial\" , 30 )).pack(padx= 40 , pady= 10 ) def tick (): global job n. set (n.get() + 1 ) job = root.after( 200 , tick) def start (): if job is None : tick() def stop (): global job if job is not None : root.after_cancel(job) job = None tk.Button(root, text= \"Start\" , command=start).pack(side= \"left\" , padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) tk.Button(root, text= \"Stop\" , command=stop).pack(side= \"left\" , pady= 10 ) root.mainloop() Pressing Start twice without the if job is None check starts two loops that run at double speed."},{"id":"tk.clock","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I show a live clock (current time)?","qh":"How do I show a live clock (current time)?","a":"Update a label with <code>time.strftime(&quot;%H:%M:%S&quot;)</code> every second using <code>after</code>.","aka":"digital clock, show time, current time label, live time, time display, date and time label","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> time\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Clock&quot;</i>)\nclock = tk.Label(root, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Courier&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">48</i>), bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;black&quot;</i>, fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffb000&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nclock.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">update</i>():\n    clock.config(text=time.strftime(<i class=\"s\">&quot;%H:%M:%S&quot;</i>))\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">1000</i>, update)\n\nupdate()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>Use <code>%I:%M %p</code> for 12-hour time with AM/PM, <code>%A %d %B %Y</code> for a date like \"Monday 05 June 2026\".</p>","see":["py.datetime-format"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import time root = tk.Tk() root.title( \"Clock\" ) clock = tk.Label(root, font=( \"Courier\" , 48 ), bg= \"black\" , fg= \"#ffb000\" , padx= 20 ) clock.pack() def update (): clock.config(text=time.strftime( \"%H:%M:%S\" )) root.after( 1000 , update) update() root.mainloop() Use %I:%M %p for 12-hour time with AM/PM, %A %d %B %Y for a date like \"Monday 05 June 2026\"."},{"id":"tk.game-loop","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I make a game loop / animation in tkinter?","qh":"How do I make a game loop / animation in tkinter?","a":"One function that updates positions, then calls <code>root.after(16, itself)</code> (about 60 frames/second). Move canvas items with <code>move()</code> or <code>coords()</code>.","aka":"game loop, animation loop, move object continuously, fps, frame rate, animate canvas, bouncing ball","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nW, H = <i class=\"n\">400</i>, <i class=\"n\">260</i>\nroot = tk.Tk()\nc = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#111&quot;</i>)\nc.pack()\nball = c.create_oval(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, <i class=\"n\">20</i>, fill=<i class=\"s\">&quot;#ffb000&quot;</i>)\nvx, vy = <i class=\"n\">4</i>, <i class=\"n\">3</i>\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">frame</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">global</i> vx, vy\n    c.move(ball, vx, vy)\n    x1, y1, x2, y2 = c.coords(ball)\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> x1 &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> x2 &gt;= W:\n        vx = -vx\n    <i class=\"k\">if</i> y1 &lt;= <i class=\"n\">0</i> <i class=\"k\">or</i> y2 &gt;= H:\n        vy = -vy\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">16</i>, frame)\n\nframe()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":["tk.canvas-move","tk.key-held","tk.recipe-snake"],"t":"import tkinter as tk W, H = 400 , 260 root = tk.Tk() c = tk.Canvas(root, width=W, height=H, bg= \"#111\" ) c.pack() ball = c.create_oval( 0 , 0 , 20 , 20 , fill= \"#ffb000\" ) vx, vy = 4 , 3 def frame (): global vx, vy c.move(ball, vx, vy) x1, y1, x2, y2 = c.coords(ball) if x1 <= 0 or x2 >= W: vx = -vx if y1 <= 0 or y2 >= H: vy = -vy root.after( 16 , frame) frame() root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.update","s":"tk-time","q":"What do update() and update_idletasks() do? Should I use them?","qh":"What do update() and update_idletasks() do? Should I use them?","a":"<code>update_idletasks()</code> redraws pending changes now (safe). <code>update()</code> also handles clicks and keys, which can re-enter your code in confusing ways. Prefer <code>after()</code> over loops with <code>update()</code>.","aka":"update, update_idletasks, refresh window, force redraw, root.update loop","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">slow_work</i>():\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> step <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">6</i>):\n        lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Step </i>{step}<i class=\"s\"> of 5</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n        lab.update_idletasks()        <i class=\"c\"># show the text now</i>\n        <i class=\"b\">sum</i>(<i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">300000</i>))            <i class=\"c\"># pretend work</i>\n\ntk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Run&quot;</i>, command=slow_work).pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">100</i>, slow_work)\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p>The window still can't be moved or clicked during the loop. For real work use a <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">thread</a>.</p>","see":["tk.after-not-sleep"],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"\" , width= 30 ) lab.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 20 ) def slow_work (): for step in range ( 1 , 6 ): lab.config(text= f\" Step {step} of 5 \" ) lab.update_idletasks() # show the text now sum ( range ( 300000 )) # pretend work tk.Button(root, text= \"Run\" , command=slow_work).pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) root.after( 100 , slow_work) root.mainloop() The window still can't be moved or clicked during the loop. For real work use a thread ."},{"id":"tk.thread","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I run a long task (download, calculation) without freezing the window?","qh":"How do I run a long task (download, calculation) without freezing the window?","a":"Run the work in a <code>threading.Thread</code>. <b>Don't touch widgets from that thread.</b> Send results back through a <code>queue.Queue</code> and read it with <code>after()</code> in the main thread.","aka":"threading tkinter, background task, long running task, freeze during download, worker thread, non blocking, progress while working","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n<i class=\"k\">import</i> threading, queue, time\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nstatus = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Idle&quot;</i>, width=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nstatus.pack(padx=<i class=\"n\">10</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">10</i>)\nq = queue.Queue()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">work</i>():                       <i class=\"c\"># runs in the background thread</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">for</i> i <i class=\"k\">in</i> <i class=\"b\">range</i>(<i class=\"n\">1</i>, <i class=\"n\">4</i>):\n        time.sleep(<i class=\"n\">0.05</i>)          <i class=\"c\"># pretend slow work</i>\n        q.put(<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Step </i>{i}<i class=\"s\"> done</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>)\n    q.put(<i class=\"s\">&quot;DONE&quot;</i>)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">poll</i>():                       <i class=\"c\"># runs in the main thread</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">try</i>:\n        <i class=\"k\">while</i> <i class=\"k\">True</i>:\n            msg = q.get_nowait()\n            status.config(text=msg)\n            <i class=\"k\">if</i> msg == <i class=\"s\">&quot;DONE&quot;</i>:\n                btn.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;normal&quot;</i>)\n                <i class=\"k\">return</i>\n    <i class=\"k\">except</i> queue.Empty:\n        <i class=\"k\">pass</i>\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">50</i>, poll)\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">start</i>():\n    btn.config(state=<i class=\"s\">&quot;disabled&quot;</i>)\n    threading.Thread(target=work, daemon=<i class=\"k\">True</i>).start()\n    poll()\n\nbtn = tk.Button(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;Start&quot;</i>, command=start)\nbtn.pack(pady=(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"n\">10</i>))\nstart()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure><p><code>daemon=True</code> lets the program quit even if the thread is still running.</p>","see":["tk.err.main-thread","tk.progressbar"],"t":"import tkinter as tk import threading, queue, time root = tk.Tk() status = tk.Label(root, text= \"Idle\" , width= 30 ) status.pack(padx= 10 , pady= 10 ) q = queue.Queue() def work (): # runs in the background thread for i in range ( 1 , 4 ): time.sleep( 0.05 ) # pretend slow work q.put( f\" Step {i} done \" ) q.put( \"DONE\" ) def poll (): # runs in the main thread try : while True : msg = q.get_nowait() status.config(text=msg) if msg == \"DONE\" : btn.config(state= \"normal\" ) return except queue.Empty: pass root.after( 50 , poll) def start (): btn.config(state= \"disabled\" ) threading.Thread(target=work, daemon= True ).start() poll() btn = tk.Button(root, text= \"Start\" , command=start) btn.pack(pady=( 0 , 10 )) start() root.mainloop() daemon=True lets the program quit even if the thread is still running."},{"id":"tk.err.main-thread","s":"tk-time","q":"RuntimeError: main thread is not in main loop","qh":"RuntimeError: main thread is not in main loop","a":"A background thread touched a widget (or tkinter object) directly. Only the main thread may use tkinter. Pass data back with a queue and <code>after()</code>, as in <a href=\"#/a/tk.thread\">How do I run a long task (download, calculation) without freezing the window?</a>.","aka":"main thread is not in main loop, tkinter thread error, Tcl_AsyncDelete, calling tkinter from thread, update gui from thread","err":"main thread is not in main loop|Tcl_AsyncDelete: async handler deleted by the wrong thread|Calling Tcl from different apartment","h":"<p>A quick alternative for small updates is <code>root.after(0, func)</code> from the thread: it schedules <code>func</code> on the main loop. This usually works in practice, but a queue is the safe way.</p>","see":["tk.thread"],"t":"A quick alternative for small updates is root.after(0, func) from the thread: it schedules func on the main loop. This usually works in practice, but a queue is the safe way."},{"id":"tk.after-idle","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I run code right after the window has appeared?","qh":"How do I run code right after the window has appeared?","a":"<code>root.after(0, func)</code> or <code>root.after_idle(func)</code> runs once <code>mainloop()</code> has started and the window is drawn.","aka":"after_idle, run after window shows, on startup, after window loaded, after mainloop starts","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;loading...&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">40</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">30</i>)\nlab.pack()\nroot.after(<i class=\"n\">0</i>, <i class=\"k\">lambda</i>: lab.config(text=<i class=\"s\">f&quot;</i><i class=\"s\">Window is </i>{root.winfo_width()}<i class=\"s\"> px wide</i><i class=\"s\">&quot;</i>))\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"loading...\" , padx= 40 , pady= 30 ) lab.pack() root.after( 0 , lambda : lab.config(text= f\" Window is {root.winfo_width()} px wide \" )) root.mainloop()"},{"id":"tk.blink","s":"tk-time","q":"How do I make text blink or flash?","qh":"How do I make text blink or flash?","a":"Toggle a colour with <code>after()</code>.","aka":"blink label, flashing text, flash color, alternate colors","err":"","h":"<figure class=\"code\"><pre><i class=\"k\">import</i> tkinter <i class=\"k\">as</i> tk\n\nroot = tk.Tk()\nlab = tk.Label(root, text=<i class=\"s\">&quot;ALERT&quot;</i>, font=(<i class=\"s\">&quot;Arial&quot;</i>, <i class=\"n\">24</i>, <i class=\"s\">&quot;bold&quot;</i>), fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i>, padx=<i class=\"n\">30</i>, pady=<i class=\"n\">20</i>)\nlab.pack()\n\n<i class=\"k\">def</i> <i class=\"f\">blink</i>(on=<i class=\"k\">True</i>):\n    lab.config(fg=<i class=\"s\">&quot;red&quot;</i> <i class=\"k\">if</i> on <i class=\"k\">else</i> root[<i class=\"s\">&quot;bg&quot;</i>])\n    root.after(<i class=\"n\">400</i>, blink, <i class=\"k\">not</i> on)\n\nblink()\nroot.mainloop()</pre></figure>","see":[],"t":"import tkinter as tk root = tk.Tk() lab = tk.Label(root, text= \"ALERT\" , font=( \"Arial\" , 24 , \"bold\" ), fg= \"red\" , padx= 30 , pady= 20 ) lab.pack() def blink (on= True ): lab.config(fg= \"red\" if on else root[ \"bg\" ]) root.after( 400 , blink, not on) blink() root.mainloop()"}]};
